1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:18,251
We're here today with
director Chang Hang-jun and J. Y. Park.

2
00:00:18,318 --> 00:00:20,487
It's the 33rd year
since J. Y. Park's debut.

3
00:00:20,553 --> 00:00:21,654
MADE HIS SOLO DEBUT IN 1994

4
00:00:21,721 --> 00:00:24,024
Over those 30 years…

5
00:00:24,090 --> 00:00:26,292
-Seriously.
-…he has produced many artists,

6
00:00:26,359 --> 00:00:29,763
and recently,
he's been taking part in state affairs.

7
00:00:29,829 --> 00:00:32,032
-Many people are quite curious.
-Right?

8
00:00:32,098 --> 00:00:33,700
So can you tell us exactly what you do?

9
00:00:33,767 --> 00:00:36,970
It's called the Presidential Committee
on Popular Culture Exchange.

10
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:39,039
We're currently planning
a K-culture festival

11
00:00:39,105 --> 00:00:41,775
that'll launch
on December 2nd of next year.

12
00:00:41,841 --> 00:00:43,343
-A festival?
-I see.

13
00:00:43,410 --> 00:00:46,713
It's a festival and an awards show
titled Fanomenon.

14
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:50,683
We took the word "phenomenon"
and changed the first part to "fan."

15
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:53,053
It means a phenomenon created by fans.

16
00:00:53,119 --> 00:00:55,655
We plan to award the fans
in different cultural fields,

17
00:00:55,722 --> 00:00:58,258
such as K-pop, K-film, and K-drama.

18
00:00:59,759 --> 00:01:00,727
That sounds fun.

19
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:01,961
TOTALLY!

20
00:01:02,028 --> 00:01:05,899
If we systematize this,
it'll become something we can pass on.

21
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:09,869
While we waited briefly outside,

22
00:01:09,936 --> 00:01:12,305
I listened to what he had to say.

23
00:01:12,372 --> 00:01:14,507
The whole project is huge,

24
00:01:14,574 --> 00:01:18,812
and I was there thinking,
"Why hadn't anyone thought of this?"

25
00:01:18,878 --> 00:01:20,380
I thought he was perfect for the job.

26
00:01:20,447 --> 00:01:21,915
If someone hears him in passing,

27
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:24,417
they'll think he sounds
like a typical con man.

28
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:25,485
Yes, you're right!

29
00:01:25,785 --> 00:01:27,253
RATHER SUSPICIOUS

30
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:30,123
-If it weren't J. Y. Park, that is.
-Yes.

31
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:32,425
But when he said it…

32
00:01:32,492 --> 00:01:33,593
It sounds credible.

33
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:35,128
Yes, I'm looking forward to it.

34
00:01:35,195 --> 00:01:36,329
I personally want to ask you

35
00:01:36,396 --> 00:01:37,730
to throw in soccer too.

36
00:01:37,797 --> 00:01:39,399
I'm not in charge of sports.

37
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:40,767
WHAT A SHAME

38
00:01:40,834 --> 00:01:42,936
<i>Chef & My Fridge</i> should join it.

39
00:01:43,002 --> 00:01:44,070
There is a cooking part.

40
00:01:44,137 --> 00:01:45,738
OH MY, CAN WE JOIN?

41
00:01:45,805 --> 00:01:48,108
-We can join.
-It'll truly be a global festival.

42
00:01:48,174 --> 00:01:50,143
In the midst of your busy schedule,

43
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:53,513
-he even released a new song.
-Really?

44
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:54,414
Tell us about it.

45
00:01:54,481 --> 00:01:55,882
It's called "WET."

46
00:01:55,949 --> 00:01:58,384
-"WET"?
-Like soaked?

47
00:01:58,451 --> 00:02:00,353
Soaked. W-E-T, wet.

48
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,889
It's like, "Make me covered in sweat."

49
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:04,357
THE MEANING BEHIND THE LYRICS

50
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:05,925
I'm not saying

51
00:02:05,992 --> 00:02:07,760
it has a dirty meaning.

52
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,529
-We weren't thinking that.
-No one said that.

53
00:02:09,596 --> 00:02:11,831
-Your clothes get wet when you work out.
-Yes.

54
00:02:11,898 --> 00:02:15,835
-That's it.
-"Let me dance and have fun."

55
00:02:15,902 --> 00:02:17,871
AN UPBEAT DANCE TRACK FOR THE SUMMER

56
00:02:17,937 --> 00:02:19,973
I heard you released "WET"

57
00:02:20,039 --> 00:02:23,209
solely for a single performance.

58
00:02:23,276 --> 00:02:25,578
Which one are you referring to?

59
00:02:25,645 --> 00:02:27,614
I'm probably performing right now

60
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:29,716
as this show is airing.

61
00:02:29,782 --> 00:02:31,050
-What?
-I see.

62
00:02:31,117 --> 00:02:32,018
WATERBOMB Festival.

63
00:02:33,887 --> 00:02:38,358
As a man in his fifties, I was surprised
when I got the offer last year.

64
00:02:38,424 --> 00:02:41,828
So I asked my agency,
"Do I say yes? Wouldn't I seem too silly?"

65
00:02:42,395 --> 00:02:43,630
But everyone told me to go.

66
00:02:43,696 --> 00:02:46,866
So I did, and the response
was much more positive than I thought.

67
00:02:46,933 --> 00:02:50,436
This year, the festival wanted
to use my song as the campaign song.

68
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:51,804
Really?

69
00:02:51,871 --> 00:02:53,706
The thing is that

70
00:02:53,773 --> 00:02:57,210
he holds the record
for the oldest performer of the festival.

71
00:02:57,277 --> 00:02:58,611
THE OLDEST ON CHEF & MY FRIDGE

72
00:02:58,678 --> 00:03:01,314
-And that's not an easy feat.
-It's not.

73
00:03:01,381 --> 00:03:04,918
So we prepared a video
of his performance there

74
00:03:04,984 --> 00:03:06,352
from last year.

75
00:03:06,419 --> 00:03:08,388
-The pride of those in their 50s.
-Yes.

76
00:03:08,454 --> 00:03:10,223
What was his performance like last year?

77
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:12,325
WATERBOMB FESTIVAL

78
00:03:12,392 --> 00:03:14,294
WATERBOMB Festival, the peak of summer.

79
00:03:14,661 --> 00:03:17,197
WALKS UP AS JYPINK

80
00:03:18,097 --> 00:03:19,132
WHOA

81
00:03:22,335 --> 00:03:25,071
LET'S GET THE PLASTIC PANTS GOING

82
00:03:25,905 --> 00:03:27,073
WEARING A HALTERNECK TOP

83
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:28,341
How do you like my back?

84
00:03:29,576 --> 00:03:30,577
I love it.

85
00:03:30,643 --> 00:03:31,678
IT'S AWESOME

86
00:03:33,313 --> 00:03:35,615
OWNED THE WATERBOMB FESTIVAL

87
00:03:38,585 --> 00:03:39,919
I want to go and see it.

88
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:41,688
AN IMPRESSIVE PERFORMANCE

89
00:03:41,754 --> 00:03:44,257
-You're in great shape.
-Totally

90
00:03:44,324 --> 00:03:46,426
He must put in a lot of effort.

91
00:03:46,492 --> 00:03:48,661
Was it your idea to wear that outfit?

92
00:03:48,728 --> 00:03:52,565
I thought everyone would expect me
to show up in plastic pants,

93
00:03:52,632 --> 00:03:55,101
so I asked people on my social media,
"What should I wear?"

94
00:03:55,168 --> 00:03:56,903
People began to comment things like,

95
00:03:56,970 --> 00:03:58,738
"You already know"

96
00:03:58,805 --> 00:04:01,074
-and "That one."
-"That one."

97
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:05,178
About 97% of the comments
said plastic pants.

98
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:07,880
But a plastic outfit is tough to dance in,

99
00:04:07,947 --> 00:04:10,250
so I decided to wear a halterneck top.

100
00:04:10,316 --> 00:04:11,251
That was why.

101
00:04:11,317 --> 00:04:13,019
He had a reason.

102
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:14,187
This year,

103
00:04:14,254 --> 00:04:15,255
you can look forward to it.

104
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:18,191
SO CURIOUS

105
00:04:18,257 --> 00:04:19,492
I'm using a new material.

106
00:04:19,559 --> 00:04:20,860
WILL USE A NEW MATERIAL THIS YEAR

107
00:04:20,927 --> 00:04:22,862
May I make a suggestion?

108
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:24,631
Make it clear like plastic

109
00:04:24,697 --> 00:04:26,466
but use thin plastic wrap instead.

110
00:04:26,532 --> 00:04:27,533
I'm sorry.

111
00:04:28,401 --> 00:04:30,069
So do you cut out comments like those?

112
00:04:30,136 --> 00:04:31,137
Of course.

113
00:04:31,204 --> 00:04:32,405
-"Of course."
-It's too…

114
00:04:32,472 --> 00:04:34,774
-That comment was useless.
-But it can survive

115
00:04:34,841 --> 00:04:36,276
if I put in my two cents.

116
00:04:37,443 --> 00:04:40,213
First, put the wrap on your legs, right?

117
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:41,648
When he mentioned the plastic wrap,

118
00:04:41,714 --> 00:04:43,016
I considered chiming in.

119
00:04:45,318 --> 00:04:47,320
Can you chime in and save me?

120
00:04:47,387 --> 00:04:49,155
Here's something I thought of.

121
00:04:49,222 --> 00:04:50,089
CAN THE LEGEND SAVE HIM?

122
00:04:50,156 --> 00:04:53,293
First, put on something
more conservative than usual

123
00:04:53,359 --> 00:04:55,828
when you make an appearance,
which turns out to be made of

124
00:04:55,895 --> 00:04:57,063
thin calligraphy paper.

125
00:04:57,697 --> 00:04:58,698
OH, MY GOSH

126
00:04:58,765 --> 00:05:00,466
And it rips when it gets wet.

127
00:05:01,134 --> 00:05:02,101
DIRECTOR HANG-JUN…

128
00:05:02,168 --> 00:05:03,403
It'll melt and disappear.

129
00:05:03,469 --> 00:05:06,406
Calligraphy paper will melt
when they get wet.

130
00:05:06,472 --> 00:05:08,007
And he can have plastic wrap inside.

131
00:05:08,241 --> 00:05:11,044
THANKS TO HANG-JUN,
THE PLASTIC WRAP TALK IS BACK

132
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:12,211
He saved you.

133
00:05:12,378 --> 00:05:13,813
PHEW

134
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,715
-He's already performing by now.
-Right.

135
00:05:15,782 --> 00:05:18,051
-So maybe next year.
-Next year.

136
00:05:18,117 --> 00:05:19,652
This year, I found the material

137
00:05:19,719 --> 00:05:22,088
in our day-to-day life.

138
00:05:22,155 --> 00:05:23,356
-I see.
-What?

139
00:05:23,423 --> 00:05:26,125
You'll often see it on windows.

140
00:05:26,326 --> 00:05:28,027
WINDOWS?

141
00:05:28,094 --> 00:05:31,297
-Mosquito net?
-Mosquito net?

142
00:05:31,931 --> 00:05:33,333
Did you bring it?

143
00:05:33,399 --> 00:05:34,567
-What is it?
-Wait.

144
00:05:36,602 --> 00:05:37,904
-What is it?
-Wait.

145
00:05:37,970 --> 00:05:40,406
INTENSELY ORANGE NET

146
00:05:40,473 --> 00:05:42,008
Is that a mesh bag for onions?

147
00:05:42,075 --> 00:05:43,509
-It is.
-It's lacy.

148
00:05:43,576 --> 00:05:45,978
SHOCKED

149
00:05:46,045 --> 00:05:48,214
This is the top. What's important is this.

150
00:05:48,281 --> 00:05:51,884
I think I've seen this
on <i>Believe It or Not!</i> type of show.

151
00:05:51,951 --> 00:05:53,152
-And his pants.
-Of course.

152
00:05:53,219 --> 00:05:55,054
It'd make international news briefs.

153
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:57,523
ENTIRELY MADE OF MESH

154
00:05:57,590 --> 00:06:00,693
-This is the whole outfit?
-Yes.

155
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:02,428
DAZED…

156
00:06:02,495 --> 00:06:04,364
I'm wearing pants with slits over these,

157
00:06:04,430 --> 00:06:07,533
-so you can see these through them.
-Through the opening.

158
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:09,502
RELIEVED

159
00:06:09,569 --> 00:06:11,504
So they peek out.

160
00:06:11,571 --> 00:06:13,740
Use them as a mosquito net after the show.

161
00:06:13,806 --> 00:06:15,041
RECYCLABLE AS A NET

162
00:06:15,675 --> 00:06:18,311
-Directors often make pledges, right?
-How cool.

163
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:19,612
-Yes.
-Pledges for…

164
00:06:19,679 --> 00:06:21,781
HANG-JUN HELD A FREE COFFEE TRUCK EVENT

165
00:06:21,848 --> 00:06:22,882
…the number of admissions.

166
00:06:22,949 --> 00:06:25,451
So if your next project also surpasses

167
00:06:25,518 --> 00:06:27,019
ten million admissions…

168
00:06:27,086 --> 00:06:28,654
-Again?
-You just saw

169
00:06:28,721 --> 00:06:31,090
J. Y. Park's outfit with us, right?

170
00:06:31,157 --> 00:06:32,658
THE MOSQUITO NET REAPPEARS

171
00:06:32,725 --> 00:06:36,929
Why don't you put that on
and go to the WATERBOMB Festival?

172
00:06:40,066 --> 00:06:41,367
He may not be into the festival,

173
00:06:41,434 --> 00:06:44,103
so wear this and give out coffee
in Gwanghwamun Square.

174
00:06:44,370 --> 00:06:46,406
THANK YOU FOR TEN MILLION ADMISSIONS

175
00:06:46,472 --> 00:06:47,707
-Let's do that.
-Okay.

176
00:06:47,774 --> 00:06:51,277
-I'll send it right over at ten million.
-If it happens.

177
00:06:52,078 --> 00:06:54,714
What I'm questioning the most right now

178
00:06:54,781 --> 00:06:56,983
is why J. Y. Park seems
so excited about this.

179
00:06:58,084 --> 00:07:00,887
It's a shame
to let it go to waste after one use.

180
00:07:00,953 --> 00:07:02,855
There's no way it'll hit ten million.

181
00:07:02,922 --> 00:07:05,491
If it does, just attend
a post-screening meet-and-greet.

182
00:07:05,558 --> 00:07:07,460
-And wear that.
-Wearing that?

183
00:07:07,527 --> 00:07:09,996
-That's better than the festival.
-Is the meet-and-greet better?

184
00:07:10,062 --> 00:07:14,233
Not a single person here
is trying to put themselves in my shoes.

185
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,835
THE MALTESE IS ANGRY

186
00:07:15,902 --> 00:07:18,504
I would dance around naked at ten million!

187
00:07:18,571 --> 00:07:20,807
I would even play soccer in that
if I could.

188
00:07:20,873 --> 00:07:21,707
TEASING

189
00:07:21,774 --> 00:07:23,209
I just physically can't.

190
00:07:23,276 --> 00:07:24,177
I see.

191
00:07:24,243 --> 00:07:26,379
Why don't we put that on and cook all day?

192
00:07:26,446 --> 00:07:27,547
THERE'S ZERO DEMAND FOR IT

193
00:07:27,613 --> 00:07:29,182
In that case…

194
00:07:29,248 --> 00:07:30,516
In that case,

195
00:07:30,583 --> 00:07:33,519
-I'll be at the theater in that.
-At the theater. That's fine, right?

196
00:07:33,853 --> 00:07:37,857
HANG-JUN PLEDGES TO DO A FANMEET IN
J. Y. PARK'S OUTFIT AFTER 10M ADMISSIONS

197
00:07:37,924 --> 00:07:38,758
That's sealed.

198
00:07:38,825 --> 00:07:40,760
I want to leave you with one thing.

199
00:07:40,827 --> 00:07:42,028
No, thanks. That's all.

200
00:07:43,329 --> 00:07:44,897
CAN'T TAKE IT BACK

201
00:07:44,964 --> 00:07:46,799
You can't take it back now.

202
00:07:46,866 --> 00:07:48,468
I'll accept my fate.

203
00:07:48,534 --> 00:07:50,002
ACCEPTS IT LIKE A CHILL GUY

204
00:07:51,404 --> 00:07:53,406
Like how Hang-jun has had
Poong in his life,

205
00:07:53,473 --> 00:07:55,942
J. Y. Park claims that he has brought up

206
00:07:56,008 --> 00:07:57,543
one of the chefs here.

207
00:07:58,845 --> 00:08:00,179
-What?
-Don't you produce singers?

208
00:08:00,246 --> 00:08:02,315
-Do you also produce chefs?
-Who?

209
00:08:02,381 --> 00:08:03,282
I've never heard it.

210
00:08:03,349 --> 00:08:05,518
Who's the chef that you brought up?

211
00:08:07,086 --> 00:08:07,920
IT'S SON JONG-WON?

212
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:10,056
-Really?
-Are you serious?

213
00:08:10,122 --> 00:08:12,258
I don't know how he did it, but great job.

214
00:08:12,325 --> 00:08:16,429
Back when nobody knew him,
when not many people knew of him,

215
00:08:16,496 --> 00:08:18,130
I ate at his restaurant and said,

216
00:08:18,197 --> 00:08:19,065
"Who is this guy?"

217
00:08:20,766 --> 00:08:22,168
"I love this guy."

218
00:08:22,235 --> 00:08:25,238
-And I took his number.
-"I love this guy."

219
00:08:25,304 --> 00:08:27,573
I'm not the type who enjoys fine dining,

220
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:28,975
but when I went

221
00:08:29,041 --> 00:08:32,478
to his French restaurant,
I knew he was something special.

222
00:08:32,545 --> 00:08:35,414
-He's the only chef I know the number of.
-Really?

223
00:08:35,481 --> 00:08:36,549
Really?

224
00:08:36,616 --> 00:08:37,783
There's no one else.

225
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:40,419
This was way before
the first season of <i>Culinary Class Wars.</i>

226
00:08:41,554 --> 00:08:44,123
And I told him
to compete in the second season.

227
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:45,791
That he had to.

228
00:08:45,858 --> 00:08:46,859
Why wouldn't he?

229
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:51,297
You all may not have known him
if it hadn't been for me.

230
00:08:51,764 --> 00:08:53,266
AMAZING…

231
00:08:53,332 --> 00:08:54,367
WHAT'S HE SAYING?

232
00:08:54,433 --> 00:08:56,402
We brought Jong-won up.

233
00:08:57,136 --> 00:08:59,639
-Yes.
-He was peeling garlic abroad back then.

234
00:08:59,705 --> 00:09:02,808
BACK IN 2016, SAN FRANCISCO…

235
00:09:02,875 --> 00:09:04,644
<i>-He's good at it.</i>
<i>-He is.</i>

236
00:09:04,710 --> 00:09:07,446
-We showed him support.
-It was way back when.

237
00:09:07,513 --> 00:09:09,181
-Was it San Francisco?
-It was.

238
00:09:09,248 --> 00:09:11,517
-You were peeling garlic cloves.
-Back in 2016.

239
00:09:11,584 --> 00:09:14,754
Back when he was peeling peas?

240
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:16,389
On <i>Cook Representative.</i>

241
00:09:16,455 --> 00:09:19,425
We practically incubated the guy.

242
00:09:19,492 --> 00:09:21,794
I go out and tell people
that I brought him up.

243
00:09:22,061 --> 00:09:23,195
MY SON, JONG-WON?

244
00:09:23,262 --> 00:09:25,398
Our production crew says that.

245
00:09:25,464 --> 00:09:27,199
You brought up what I had brought up.

246
00:09:27,266 --> 00:09:28,801
Is that the case?

247
00:09:28,868 --> 00:09:31,571
Everyone is trying to take the credit.

248
00:09:31,637 --> 00:09:33,172
We need him to admit to this.

249
00:09:33,239 --> 00:09:35,207
-Yes.
-That's true.

250
00:09:35,274 --> 00:09:36,943
Do you agree with any of this?

251
00:09:37,009 --> 00:09:39,679
Recently, I've definitely been hearing
many people say

252
00:09:39,745 --> 00:09:42,181
that they brought me up.

253
00:09:42,248 --> 00:09:44,083
-Yes, right?
-We did.

254
00:09:44,150 --> 00:09:46,619
I believe my mother
was the one who brought me up.

255
00:09:46,686 --> 00:09:47,587
-True.
-So it was your mother.

256
00:09:47,653 --> 00:09:49,956
-It was his mother.
-It was.

257
00:09:50,022 --> 00:09:53,392
-But there is a connection.
-I'm very grateful.

258
00:09:53,459 --> 00:09:55,528
-Wait, what?
-I can't dance.

259
00:09:55,595 --> 00:09:57,129
There's disconcerting information.

260
00:09:57,196 --> 00:09:58,097
-What?
-What?

261
00:09:58,164 --> 00:10:02,401
J. Y. Park said he'd send our crew
a picture with information about his body,

262
00:10:03,102 --> 00:10:04,837
and he insisted

263
00:10:04,904 --> 00:10:07,239
that we show it to the chefs
before they start cooking.

264
00:10:07,306 --> 00:10:08,474
-What?
-His body?

265
00:10:08,541 --> 00:10:12,511
-I have to share my order soon, right?
-Yes.

266
00:10:12,578 --> 00:10:14,580
This information
is directly related to that.

267
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:16,382
A-HA

268
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:18,351
-Something he can't eat?
-All right.

269
00:10:18,417 --> 00:10:20,786
-Please take a look.
-Okay.

270
00:10:20,853 --> 00:10:23,022
I hear the production crew shrieked
after seeing this.

271
00:10:23,089 --> 00:10:24,991
-They did? Why?
-What?

272
00:10:25,057 --> 00:10:27,393
We haven't seen it yet.

273
00:10:27,693 --> 00:10:29,061
J. Y. PARK'S BODY PORTRAIT

274
00:10:29,128 --> 00:10:30,663
Is this a recent photo?

275
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:35,101
Yes, I actually only had 9.2% of body fat.

276
00:10:35,167 --> 00:10:37,703
-Yes, "body fat, 9.2%."
-Look at his body fat.

277
00:10:38,037 --> 00:10:39,772
WHAT NOW?

278
00:10:39,839 --> 00:10:42,975
I can't eat carbs
for the next three months.

279
00:10:43,042 --> 00:10:44,977
I would die. I can't live like that.

280
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:46,212
How will he eat, then?

281
00:10:46,278 --> 00:10:50,583
So you've actually hit
this 9.2% body fat before?

282
00:10:50,650 --> 00:10:52,451
I usually maintain around 12 to 13%,

283
00:10:52,518 --> 00:10:55,221
and recently, I tried going down to that.

284
00:10:55,287 --> 00:10:56,789
AMAZED

285
00:10:56,856 --> 00:10:59,458
I promised my fans

286
00:10:59,525 --> 00:11:02,862
that I'd show them
my best performance at 60,

287
00:11:02,928 --> 00:11:04,664
which is now only five years away.

288
00:11:04,730 --> 00:11:08,968
So I've been getting in shape
to do a great job for the last five years.

289
00:11:09,035 --> 00:11:11,170
It was a promise to his fans.

290
00:11:11,237 --> 00:11:13,539
In some respects,
I did better in my twenties and thirties,

291
00:11:13,606 --> 00:11:15,107
but overall,

292
00:11:15,174 --> 00:11:16,676
I'm in my best physical condition now.

293
00:11:16,742 --> 00:11:18,144
-Compared to your younger days?
-Yes.

294
00:11:18,210 --> 00:11:19,945
I'm way more flexible now too.

295
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:24,083
Do you suffer from any chronic illnesses?

296
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:26,485
-Like blood pressure.
-Yes, blood pressure and all.

297
00:11:26,552 --> 00:11:27,620
Nothing.

298
00:11:27,687 --> 00:11:29,321
As expected.

299
00:11:29,522 --> 00:11:30,923
J. Y. PARK AGES IN REVERSE

300
00:11:30,990 --> 00:11:33,159
When you cook for J. Y. Park today,

301
00:11:33,225 --> 00:11:36,562
you must take
his physical condition into consideration.

302
00:11:36,629 --> 00:11:39,932
-You're right. That's not easy.
-That'll probably be his order.

303
00:11:39,999 --> 00:11:43,836
We're about to look into the fridge
of the man who's conditioning himself

304
00:11:43,903 --> 00:11:46,572
until the day he hits 60, J. Y. Park.

305
00:11:46,639 --> 00:11:48,674
-Do you use this with your family?
-Yes.

306
00:11:48,741 --> 00:11:51,110
With my wife and two daughters.

307
00:11:51,177 --> 00:11:54,213
You seem to pay a lot of attention
to maintaining your health.

308
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:56,716
Is it different from the fridges
in other households?

309
00:11:56,782 --> 00:11:59,618
-It's quite different.
-It is?

310
00:11:59,685 --> 00:12:01,020
-It is?
-How so?

311
00:12:01,087 --> 00:12:02,621
I once saw on TV

312
00:12:02,688 --> 00:12:04,123
that you had a morning routine…

313
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:06,358
-Yes, with supplements.
-…of working out and eating.

314
00:12:06,425 --> 00:12:08,561
Have you been keeping that up?

315
00:12:08,627 --> 00:12:09,729
As soon as you wake up?

316
00:12:09,795 --> 00:12:11,530
First, I memorize 20 English words.

317
00:12:11,831 --> 00:12:13,032
YOU'RE STUDYING ENGLISH?

318
00:12:13,099 --> 00:12:14,500
Memorizing 20 English words?

319
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:16,602
How can you memorize anything
before breakfast?

320
00:12:16,669 --> 00:12:18,804
Your brain wakes up before your body.

321
00:12:18,871 --> 00:12:20,973
My brain wakes up around 1:00 p.m.

322
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:23,509
After that, I eat breakfast,

323
00:12:23,576 --> 00:12:25,311
stretch, work out,

324
00:12:25,377 --> 00:12:28,347
then practice piano and singing.

325
00:12:28,414 --> 00:12:32,051
Also, he's famous
for his love for organic products.

326
00:12:32,118 --> 00:12:34,186
-You're right.
-The word is out there.

327
00:12:34,253 --> 00:12:36,021
Why do you insist on
using organic products?

328
00:12:36,088 --> 00:12:38,424
The artists signed with my agency

329
00:12:38,491 --> 00:12:42,595
would do something like this
whenever they'd impersonate me

330
00:12:42,661 --> 00:12:44,463
because I had severe atopic eczema.

331
00:12:45,331 --> 00:12:47,566
I'm not supposed to scratch myself,
so I slapped myself instead.

332
00:12:47,633 --> 00:12:49,201
HAD TO SLAP HIMSELF TO HANDLE THE ITCH

333
00:12:49,268 --> 00:12:50,803
I lived like that my whole life,

334
00:12:50,870 --> 00:12:53,572
and one day, I thought,
"Is there really no way?"

335
00:12:53,639 --> 00:12:57,943
So I began to study autoimmune diseases,

336
00:12:58,010 --> 00:13:00,613
and what I found was, in a nutshell,

337
00:13:00,679 --> 00:13:03,716
that I should not let
bad things enter my blood system.

338
00:13:04,416 --> 00:13:06,485
"Organic" just means

339
00:13:06,552 --> 00:13:10,790
they're free of pesticides and chemicals
and have minimal processing.

340
00:13:10,856 --> 00:13:13,125
I tried doing that for three months.

341
00:13:13,192 --> 00:13:17,897
I had severe eczema and hay fever,
which usually come together.

342
00:13:17,963 --> 00:13:20,366
For the first time in 40 years,
I breathed through my nose

343
00:13:20,432 --> 00:13:21,967
in my sleep and woke up in shock.

344
00:13:22,668 --> 00:13:23,669
THAT CAN HAPPEN?

345
00:13:23,736 --> 00:13:24,570
So that can happen?

346
00:13:24,637 --> 00:13:26,705
And it's been 12 years
since I scratched myself.

347
00:13:27,039 --> 00:13:28,340
HIS ORGANIC LIFESTYLE MADE A CHANGE

348
00:13:28,407 --> 00:13:30,376
Food is the best medicine.

349
00:13:30,442 --> 00:13:35,314
That's why you said you were
in your best physical condition now.

350
00:13:35,381 --> 00:13:38,551
-Let's see how organic his fridge is.
-Let's see it.

351
00:13:38,617 --> 00:13:40,719
-I should learn from him.
-Let's look at J. Y. Park's fridge.

352
00:13:40,786 --> 00:13:41,754
I should live like that.

353
00:13:41,821 --> 00:13:45,057
-We're about to begin.
-I should copy him.

354
00:13:45,124 --> 00:13:47,092
The fridge for a family of four,

355
00:13:47,159 --> 00:13:49,595
-we present J. Y. Park's fridge!
-Fridge!

356
00:13:50,496 --> 00:13:51,497
SURPRISED

357
00:13:51,564 --> 00:13:52,998
-There's nothing.
-What's that?

358
00:13:53,065 --> 00:13:54,900
His freezer's empty.

359
00:13:55,234 --> 00:13:58,037
THE FRIDGE IS FULL

360
00:13:58,103 --> 00:14:01,040
-He has nothing in the freezer.
-Barely anything.

361
00:14:01,106 --> 00:14:01,941
It's empty.

362
00:14:02,007 --> 00:14:05,211
I can see that
his fridge must be quite healthy

363
00:14:05,277 --> 00:14:06,512
and even Westernized.

364
00:14:06,579 --> 00:14:08,714
The thing is,
he's not prepared for an emergency.

365
00:14:08,948 --> 00:14:11,383
WHAT EMERGENCY?

366
00:14:11,450 --> 00:14:13,919
He'll have to starve in case of emergency.

367
00:14:13,986 --> 00:14:16,989
Let's take a look from the top. Some eggs.

368
00:14:17,056 --> 00:14:19,091
Organic fertilized egg, free-range.

369
00:14:19,158 --> 00:14:21,026
You must eat a lot of eggs.

370
00:14:21,093 --> 00:14:23,662
After conducting
many experiments on myself,

371
00:14:23,729 --> 00:14:25,831
if I must recommend one perfect food,

372
00:14:25,898 --> 00:14:27,833
it would be eggs.

373
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:29,668
THAT'S TRUE

374
00:14:29,902 --> 00:14:31,737
However, the chickens must be free-range

375
00:14:31,804 --> 00:14:34,974
and must be fed good feed
in a good environment.

376
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:37,209
Free-range organic fertilized egg.

377
00:14:37,443 --> 00:14:39,011
ALL EARS

378
00:14:39,078 --> 00:14:40,813
I've always loved them,

379
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,982
and they're good for you too.

380
00:14:43,048 --> 00:14:46,352
So I love dishes
that use eggs as the main ingredient.

381
00:14:46,418 --> 00:14:47,853
I see.

382
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:49,555
What's your favorite egg dish?

383
00:14:49,622 --> 00:14:51,290
Wrinkly rolled omelet.

384
00:14:51,357 --> 00:14:52,358
KOREAN-STYLE, GOLDEN BROWN

385
00:14:52,424 --> 00:14:54,460
-Overcooked?
-Korean-style rolled omelet.

386
00:14:54,526 --> 00:14:57,897
-That and kimchi-<i>jjigae</i> with saury.
-That's so good.

387
00:14:57,963 --> 00:14:59,431
HE UNEXPECTEDLY LOVES KOREAN FOOD

388
00:14:59,498 --> 00:15:00,833
So eggs are a perfect food.

389
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:04,336
I see lots of cheese here.
Do you like cheese?

390
00:15:04,403 --> 00:15:06,872
Not really. I only love burrata.

391
00:15:07,106 --> 00:15:08,807
STRONG PREFERENCES

392
00:15:08,874 --> 00:15:10,843
-Burrata with ground pepper.
-That's good.

393
00:15:10,910 --> 00:15:12,177
GOSH

394
00:15:13,445 --> 00:15:14,880
-He's imagining it.
-With ground pepper.

395
00:15:14,947 --> 00:15:17,449
I like to eat that with beef tenderloin

396
00:15:17,516 --> 00:15:19,752
for lunch.

397
00:15:20,319 --> 00:15:21,787
I don't eat a huge breakfast.

398
00:15:21,854 --> 00:15:25,791
I just eat nuts, fruit, and yogurt.

399
00:15:25,858 --> 00:15:28,294
-That's all?
-No big meals.

400
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:29,662
But I eat a proper lunch.

401
00:15:29,728 --> 00:15:32,531
I only eat within a 6-hour window,
then fast for 18 hours.

402
00:15:32,598 --> 00:15:33,799
Wait.

403
00:15:33,866 --> 00:15:35,200
Do you not eat dinner?

404
00:15:35,267 --> 00:15:37,703
On days that I must eat dinner,
I skip lunch.

405
00:15:37,937 --> 00:15:39,605
THOROUGH CALORIE INTAKE CONTROL

406
00:15:39,672 --> 00:15:41,974
I can dance and work out better
when I'm on an empty stomach.

407
00:15:42,041 --> 00:15:45,344
-I hear you drink oil in the morning.
-Oil?

408
00:15:45,411 --> 00:15:49,848
I take different oils in shot glasses.

409
00:15:49,915 --> 00:15:51,684
-Different kinds?
-Not just one?

410
00:15:51,750 --> 00:15:52,584
No, not just one.

411
00:15:52,651 --> 00:15:54,053
Olive oil is a given.

412
00:15:54,119 --> 00:15:55,788
At times, I take fish oil…

413
00:15:55,854 --> 00:15:58,958
<i>-For omega-3.</i>
<i>-…or olive oil extract.</i>

414
00:15:59,024 --> 00:16:01,427
You're right. These are all organic too.

415
00:16:01,493 --> 00:16:03,162
Even his ground sesame and salt
are organic.

416
00:16:03,228 --> 00:16:05,531
-These are organic too.
-You're right.

417
00:16:05,597 --> 00:16:07,132
Even toasted sesame.

418
00:16:07,199 --> 00:16:08,701
-And ground pepper.
-Ground pepper.

419
00:16:08,767 --> 00:16:10,302
ORGANIC

420
00:16:10,369 --> 00:16:12,237
Organic sesame oil.
Organic green plum extract.

421
00:16:12,304 --> 00:16:15,341
Hang-jun didn't eat fruit,

422
00:16:15,407 --> 00:16:17,509
but you have some, including green grapes.

423
00:16:17,576 --> 00:16:20,713
Blueberries, lemons, and apples.

424
00:16:20,779 --> 00:16:21,914
Do you enjoy fruit?

425
00:16:21,981 --> 00:16:23,349
I only eat them in the morning.

426
00:16:23,415 --> 00:16:25,918
Hang-jun, as we age,

427
00:16:25,985 --> 00:16:28,053
we have to eat fruit for enzymes.

428
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,823
-Right.
-Enzymes in fruit…

429
00:16:30,889 --> 00:16:32,691
-I'll eat some.
-…are important for people.

430
00:16:32,758 --> 00:16:35,627
It works wonders for anti-aging.

431
00:16:35,694 --> 00:16:36,795
-Really?
-Really?

432
00:16:36,862 --> 00:16:38,263
ACCEPTS THE ADVICE

433
00:16:38,330 --> 00:16:41,567
-But in the morning. It's high in sugar.
-Like berries.

434
00:16:41,633 --> 00:16:43,836
-He's like a professor.
-He is.

435
00:16:43,902 --> 00:16:46,005
The second shelf has

436
00:16:46,071 --> 00:16:48,974
-lots of meat.
-That's more meat than I expected.

437
00:16:49,041 --> 00:16:50,943
Organic whole chicken.

438
00:16:51,176 --> 00:16:52,444
EVEN THAT?

439
00:16:52,511 --> 00:16:55,948
-Anything that isn't organic is banned.
-Off limits.

440
00:16:56,015 --> 00:16:58,684
And this one is antibiotic-free pork.

441
00:16:58,751 --> 00:16:59,885
If I can't find organic products,

442
00:16:59,952 --> 00:17:02,354
I go down to no antibiotics
and eco-friendly ones.

443
00:17:02,421 --> 00:17:03,889
-Pork.
-Yes.

444
00:17:03,956 --> 00:17:06,358
These all exist out there.
We just didn't know.

445
00:17:06,425 --> 00:17:08,494
You have many different pork cuts.

446
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:09,795
I love pork.

447
00:17:09,862 --> 00:17:12,364
Pork is hands down
my favorite type of meat.

448
00:17:12,431 --> 00:17:14,033
THE BEST KIND OF MEAT

449
00:17:14,099 --> 00:17:16,168
-What's this?
-Brown croaker?

450
00:17:16,235 --> 00:17:18,470
-It's probably a brown croaker.
-It's a brown croaker.

451
00:17:18,537 --> 00:17:19,772
MUST ROTATE THE SCREEN TO FIT

452
00:17:19,838 --> 00:17:20,939
-First time on the show.
-It looks fresh.

453
00:17:21,006 --> 00:17:22,674
That's a really expensive fish.

454
00:17:22,741 --> 00:17:24,810
-A ridged-eye flounder.
-Ridged-eye flounder?

455
00:17:24,877 --> 00:17:26,045
You eat that at home?

456
00:17:26,412 --> 00:17:27,279
ABALONES

457
00:17:27,346 --> 00:17:29,415
How do you eat these? They're huge.

458
00:17:29,481 --> 00:17:33,318
I try to find wild-caught fish
instead of farmed ones.

459
00:17:33,385 --> 00:17:34,787
EVEN SEAFOOD MUST BE WILD-CAUGHT

460
00:17:34,853 --> 00:17:36,955
You have lots of kimchi too.

461
00:17:37,022 --> 00:17:39,491
Green onion kimchi, cabbage kimchi,
<i>mukeunji</i>, water kimchi.

462
00:17:39,558 --> 00:17:42,628
-Do you like kimchi?
-I love them, really.

463
00:17:42,694 --> 00:17:44,696
I've loved it since two decades ago.

464
00:17:44,763 --> 00:17:46,465
I actually used to love Western cuisine.

465
00:17:46,532 --> 00:17:48,067
I see. Western cuisine.

466
00:17:48,133 --> 00:17:50,502
But between 2003 and 2008,

467
00:17:50,569 --> 00:17:54,640
<i>I went to America in an attempt</i>
<i>to sell K-pop to major music labels.</i>

468
00:17:54,706 --> 00:17:56,675
No one would even meet me,
and they didn't know

469
00:17:56,742 --> 00:17:58,177
-where Korea was.
-With Wonder Girls?

470
00:17:58,243 --> 00:18:00,379
I ended up facing
so many upsetting situations.

471
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:04,817
I'd pack my backpack with 30 CDs
and did my round with music labels,

472
00:18:04,883 --> 00:18:07,386
asking them to have a listen.

473
00:18:07,453 --> 00:18:10,556
But the response wasn't so great,

474
00:18:10,622 --> 00:18:14,193
and I started to miss Korea,
then began my obsession with Korean food.

475
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:15,928
HE ALSO BEGAN TO MISS KOREAN FOOD

476
00:18:15,994 --> 00:18:17,763
I began to crave them,

477
00:18:17,830 --> 00:18:20,599
and all I had on my mind
was kimchi and pork.

478
00:18:20,666 --> 00:18:22,501
And I began to keep many kinds of kimchi.

479
00:18:23,368 --> 00:18:24,937
-It happens.
-I get it.

480
00:18:25,003 --> 00:18:29,441
Times were pretty tough, and my love
for kimchi and pork began back then.

481
00:18:29,741 --> 00:18:32,044
K-FOOD BECAME HIS COMFORT

482
00:18:32,111 --> 00:18:35,013
Pork and kimchi saved you.

483
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:36,849
But now,

484
00:18:36,915 --> 00:18:42,020
even last week, the biggest label
in America came here for a meeting.

485
00:18:42,087 --> 00:18:44,957
-The tables have turned.
-Times have changed.

486
00:18:45,023 --> 00:18:49,995
Just last year, we earned more
in America than in Korea.

487
00:18:50,062 --> 00:18:51,363
I can clearly see why he leads

488
00:18:51,430 --> 00:18:54,133
the Presidential Committee
on Popular Culture Exchange.

489
00:18:54,199 --> 00:18:57,769
-Yes, he knows the ins and outs.
-That's why he took it up.

490
00:18:57,836 --> 00:19:01,607
Lots of veggies in the crisper drawer,
and again, organic chives.

491
00:19:01,673 --> 00:19:03,942
-These are all organic.
-Organic spinach.

492
00:19:04,009 --> 00:19:05,310
Do you enjoy vegetables?

493
00:19:05,377 --> 00:19:06,879
-I like them.
-I see.

494
00:19:06,945 --> 00:19:09,081
But for green onions…

495
00:19:09,148 --> 00:19:11,517
I put them in, then take them out later.

496
00:19:11,583 --> 00:19:13,552
-Why?
-Just the aroma?

497
00:19:13,619 --> 00:19:14,820
Yes, I like the aroma.

498
00:19:14,887 --> 00:19:16,421
It tastes weird in my mouth.

499
00:19:16,622 --> 00:19:18,357
HE DOESN'T LIKE THE TEXTURE

500
00:19:18,423 --> 00:19:20,425
But Poong uses a lot of green onion.

501
00:19:20,492 --> 00:19:23,862
A variety of vegetables
that come in different colors

502
00:19:23,929 --> 00:19:26,298
will eliminate a lot
of reactive oxygen species.

503
00:19:26,365 --> 00:19:30,169
So we must eat vegetables
in many different colors

504
00:19:30,235 --> 00:19:31,103
to age slower.

505
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:33,972
-Was it anthocyanin or something?
-Vibrant colors.

506
00:19:34,973 --> 00:19:36,475
Wait, how long do you want to live?

507
00:19:37,142 --> 00:19:38,877
WE CAN'T EVEN TELL…

508
00:19:38,944 --> 00:19:42,014
Once I show my best performance at 60,
I'll relax a little more.

509
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:44,216
-After that, will you relax?
-Yes.

510
00:19:44,283 --> 00:19:46,752
Yes, I need to keep my promise
at least until I hit 60.

511
00:19:46,818 --> 00:19:49,188
-It's to keep that.
-He really keeps his promises.

512
00:19:49,254 --> 00:19:51,323
Other than music and organic products,

513
00:19:51,390 --> 00:19:53,492
is there nothing else
you're interested in?

514
00:19:54,626 --> 00:19:56,094
No, not really.

515
00:19:56,361 --> 00:19:58,063
DETACHED

516
00:19:58,130 --> 00:20:02,000
Many others spend money
on expensive cars and watches, but not me.

517
00:20:02,067 --> 00:20:03,402
I put everything into this.

518
00:20:03,468 --> 00:20:05,103
You're kind of like me.

519
00:20:05,170 --> 00:20:07,306
I always splurge on food too.

520
00:20:07,940 --> 00:20:09,708
THAT IS NOT THE SAME!

521
00:20:09,775 --> 00:20:12,711
Was it your idea to use
only organic products for your cafeteria?

522
00:20:12,778 --> 00:20:15,214
I wouldn't be able to face my staff
if only I had good food.

523
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:18,450
<i>So my agency's cafeteria</i>
<i>is run just like how I would eat.</i>

524
00:20:18,517 --> 00:20:21,587
<i>It's for our employees,</i>
<i>singers, and even trainees.</i>

525
00:20:21,653 --> 00:20:22,487
<i>You know that it's good.</i>

526
00:20:22,554 --> 00:20:27,226
Even for cooking oil, I made sure that
we only used cold-pressed organic oil.

527
00:20:27,292 --> 00:20:30,229
He knows the importance of food.

528
00:20:30,295 --> 00:20:31,730
What's this one?

529
00:20:31,797 --> 00:20:34,433
Organic maple sugar.

530
00:20:34,499 --> 00:20:36,001
When I want to add sweetness,

531
00:20:36,068 --> 00:20:38,670
I choose something
that's the least processed,

532
00:20:38,737 --> 00:20:41,707
and maple sugar is minimally processed.

533
00:20:41,773 --> 00:20:42,941
I feel like

534
00:20:43,008 --> 00:20:45,077
I'm watching a health show right now.

535
00:20:45,310 --> 00:20:47,379
SECRETS TO ORGANIC PRODUCTS

536
00:20:47,446 --> 00:20:49,514
And everything he says
is so easy to understand.

537
00:20:49,581 --> 00:20:51,416
It's captivating too.

538
00:20:51,483 --> 00:20:53,518
My friends would make fun of me

539
00:20:53,585 --> 00:20:56,421
and say people like me
die first in accidents.

540
00:20:56,622 --> 00:20:57,489
MY GOSH

541
00:20:57,556 --> 00:20:58,490
That's a curse.

542
00:20:58,557 --> 00:21:00,659
All of them are unwell now,

543
00:21:00,726 --> 00:21:01,693
and they no longer tease me.

544
00:21:01,760 --> 00:21:03,829
HE PROVED IT HIMSELF

545
00:21:03,895 --> 00:21:05,764
What's this? Tofu noodles?

546
00:21:05,831 --> 00:21:07,366
Those are noodles made with tofu,

547
00:21:07,432 --> 00:21:11,036
so they're much lower in carbs and sugar.

548
00:21:11,103 --> 00:21:12,704
What's the texture like?

549
00:21:12,771 --> 00:21:14,973
-It's not as chewy as regular noodles.
-I see.

550
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:17,042
But it can pass.

551
00:21:17,109 --> 00:21:18,176
It's free of flour.

552
00:21:18,243 --> 00:21:20,178
My perception of organic products

553
00:21:20,245 --> 00:21:22,748
has changed completely today.

554
00:21:23,081 --> 00:21:25,317
AFTER LISTENING TO THE PROFESSOR

555
00:21:25,951 --> 00:21:28,954
Why am I taking herbal medicine?
I should use good ingredients.

556
00:21:29,021 --> 00:21:30,889
-Yes, eat organic food.
-Yes.

557
00:21:30,956 --> 00:21:32,691
I'm just an organism

558
00:21:32,758 --> 00:21:34,493
while he's organic.

559
00:21:34,559 --> 00:21:37,229
So I think I should choose

560
00:21:37,296 --> 00:21:39,765
organic products when I can.

561
00:21:40,065 --> 00:21:41,600
THE PROFESSOR IS PROUD

562
00:21:41,667 --> 00:21:43,001
In the freezer,

563
00:21:43,068 --> 00:21:44,169
there's rice cake.

564
00:21:44,236 --> 00:21:46,038
Organic rice cake.

565
00:21:46,104 --> 00:21:49,207
You're famous for your love of rice cake

566
00:21:49,274 --> 00:21:51,743
and was nicknamed the Rice Cake Gorilla.

567
00:21:51,810 --> 00:21:53,645
This is one thing you can't give up on.

568
00:21:53,712 --> 00:21:55,314
I brought it so I could have some today.

569
00:21:55,547 --> 00:21:58,317
TO COMMEMORATE HIS VISIT TO THE SHOW

570
00:21:58,383 --> 00:22:01,887
I wanted to eat something
made by excellent chefs like them.

571
00:22:02,888 --> 00:22:06,024
That's all for J. Y. Park's fridge!

572
00:22:06,491 --> 00:22:09,027
A FRIDGE ONLY FILLED WITH ORGANIC PRODUCTS

573
00:22:09,094 --> 00:22:10,762
-That was amazing.
-It was.

574
00:22:10,829 --> 00:22:14,399
He has a lot of love for organic products.

575
00:22:14,466 --> 00:22:16,835
-And we learned a few things, right?
-Yes.

576
00:22:16,902 --> 00:22:19,538
-I want to try copying him.
-We should live like him.

577
00:22:19,604 --> 00:22:21,673
What's important
isn't eating what's good for you.

578
00:22:21,740 --> 00:22:23,442
Just avoid eating what's bad for you.

579
00:22:23,508 --> 00:22:24,609
Right.

580
00:22:24,676 --> 00:22:27,312
But most people think
it's the other way around.

581
00:22:27,379 --> 00:22:30,315
What's important
is not eating what's bad for you

582
00:22:30,382 --> 00:22:32,918
but staying away from those
is the toughest.

583
00:22:32,984 --> 00:22:35,887
It's weird how the food
that's bad for you is all good.

584
00:22:35,954 --> 00:22:38,190
-They taste good.
-They're so good.

585
00:22:38,256 --> 00:22:39,391
You're right.

586
00:22:39,458 --> 00:22:41,226
Alcohol is so delicious, isn't it?

587
00:22:43,428 --> 00:22:45,030
And it makes you feel so happy.

588
00:22:45,530 --> 00:22:46,865
HAPPY

589
00:22:46,932 --> 00:22:48,200
Now, get ready.

590
00:22:48,266 --> 00:22:52,037
Let's find out
what J. Y. Park's orders are.

591
00:22:52,104 --> 00:22:54,673
Here are his orders.

592
00:22:54,740 --> 00:22:56,041
J. Y. Park's orders!

593
00:22:57,142 --> 00:22:59,010
-Peaking at 60.
-Peaking at 60.

594
00:22:59,077 --> 00:23:00,479
Peaking at 60.

595
00:23:00,545 --> 00:23:02,214
He's doing all this

596
00:23:02,280 --> 00:23:05,050
-for his performance at 60.
-For that performance.

597
00:23:05,117 --> 00:23:06,585
What are you looking to eat?

598
00:23:06,651 --> 00:23:10,756
Help me maintain this great condition
in the remaining five years

599
00:23:10,822 --> 00:23:14,793
by using sugar and carbs
as little as you can,

600
00:23:14,860 --> 00:23:16,528
and using mostly protein and veggies.

601
00:23:16,595 --> 00:23:20,365
-High protein, low calories.
-Also, I really love eggs,

602
00:23:20,432 --> 00:23:22,934
so please use eggs as the main ingredient.

603
00:23:23,001 --> 00:23:27,005
He wants you to use a lot of eggs.

604
00:23:27,072 --> 00:23:28,907
-The second order.
-The second one.

605
00:23:29,875 --> 00:23:31,510
-Half pork, half kimchi.
-Half pork, half kimchi.

606
00:23:32,077 --> 00:23:33,478
Not half sound, half air.

607
00:23:33,545 --> 00:23:35,847
-No. Half pork, half kimchi.
-Half pork, half kimchi.

608
00:23:35,914 --> 00:23:36,782
What are you looking for?

609
00:23:36,848 --> 00:23:41,019
Whenever I return from business trips
to America or any other countries,

610
00:23:41,086 --> 00:23:44,689
I always have to eat
something made with pork and kimchi.

611
00:23:44,756 --> 00:23:45,791
That sounds good.

612
00:23:45,857 --> 00:23:48,593
But I always eat the same thing,
so I wanted something new.

613
00:23:48,660 --> 00:23:49,995
For this order,

614
00:23:50,061 --> 00:23:52,464
you don't have to consider
the calories or carbs.

615
00:23:53,165 --> 00:23:55,434
Something that
makes me feel like I'm in Korea.

616
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:58,403
-It doesn't have to be organic. The oil--
-It has to be organic.

617
00:23:58,470 --> 00:24:00,105
But carbs are allowed.

618
00:24:00,172 --> 00:24:02,707
No limits on the carbs and the calories.

619
00:24:02,774 --> 00:24:05,210
-Everything you have is organic anyway.
-Yes.

620
00:24:06,077 --> 00:24:09,147
Yong-wook has joined us
since the last episode.

621
00:24:09,214 --> 00:24:11,550
-Do I get the first pick?
-Would you like to go first?

622
00:24:11,616 --> 00:24:14,953
-Why do you seem so rushed?
-I do feel rushed.

623
00:24:15,020 --> 00:24:17,923
I don't think I can do the one with eggs.

624
00:24:17,989 --> 00:24:20,025
-So the pork?
-Pork.

625
00:24:20,091 --> 00:24:22,561
-The pork round?
-If I can choose, yes.

626
00:24:22,627 --> 00:24:25,597
-Half Pork, Half Kimchi.
-Half Pork, Half Kimchi.

627
00:24:25,664 --> 00:24:27,466
-All right.
-Hey, Poong.

628
00:24:27,532 --> 00:24:29,234
You're also from the streets.

629
00:24:29,301 --> 00:24:31,903
-I am. Just like that?
-Why don't you face him?

630
00:24:31,970 --> 00:24:33,038
Streets versus streets?

631
00:24:33,104 --> 00:24:34,239
He's a tough one.

632
00:24:34,306 --> 00:24:38,009
-All right. Half Pork, Half Kimchi.
-Half Pork, Half Kimchi.

633
00:24:38,076 --> 00:24:40,545
-Thank you for joining me.
-Okay.

634
00:24:40,612 --> 00:24:42,981
The two have chosen
Half Pork, Half Kimchi,

635
00:24:43,048 --> 00:24:45,684
-which puts Jong-won and Sam up there.
-Jong-won and Sam.

636
00:24:45,750 --> 00:24:46,852
Is that okay?

637
00:24:46,918 --> 00:24:49,221
His fridge looked

638
00:24:49,287 --> 00:24:52,190
-very ideal to me. I'd be happy to join.
-Yes, he's like him.

639
00:24:52,257 --> 00:24:53,859
Sam, you must be confident about this.

640
00:24:53,925 --> 00:24:56,828
Yes, it's a chance to show
my naturalist philosophy again.

641
00:24:56,895 --> 00:24:59,397
Yes, he was originally a naturalist.

642
00:24:59,464 --> 00:25:00,999
To be exact,

643
00:25:01,066 --> 00:25:03,101
a former naturalist chef.

644
00:25:03,168 --> 00:25:06,505
He's the former naturalist chef
who has compromised quite a lot.

645
00:25:06,571 --> 00:25:08,640
Now, the first round.

646
00:25:08,707 --> 00:25:12,010
Let us begin the 15-minute cook-off
between Sam and Jong-won!

647
00:25:15,213 --> 00:25:19,417
<i>The first round, played with ingredients</i>
<i>from J. Y. Park's fridge.</i>

648
00:25:19,484 --> 00:25:21,319
Chef Son Jong-won,

649
00:25:21,386 --> 00:25:23,255
who's currently peaking
thanks to our show,

650
00:25:23,321 --> 00:25:25,390
versus Chef Sam Kim,

651
00:25:25,457 --> 00:25:28,059
who'll take Jong-won down today

652
00:25:28,126 --> 00:25:30,629
and steal that peak from him.

653
00:25:30,695 --> 00:25:32,297
These two have always shown

654
00:25:32,364 --> 00:25:34,566
a great cook-off
whenever they faced each other.

655
00:25:34,633 --> 00:25:36,968
You two have done the cook-off five times,

656
00:25:37,035 --> 00:25:40,405
and Jong-won has
won four times and lost once.

657
00:25:40,472 --> 00:25:42,040
-He won four times?
-Four times?

658
00:25:42,707 --> 00:25:46,077
However, Jong-won is facing a wall
after his ninth win.

659
00:25:46,144 --> 00:25:50,081
<i>He has lost three times in a row,</i>
<i>while trying to get his tenth star.</i>

660
00:25:50,148 --> 00:25:54,519
That's why we still don't have anyone
who has stars in the two digits.

661
00:25:54,586 --> 00:25:56,788
WE HAVE ONE WITH ONLY TWO STARS THOUGH

662
00:25:56,855 --> 00:25:58,890
Will you use Sam
as the sacrifice for the tenth win?

663
00:25:58,957 --> 00:25:59,791
Is that possible?

664
00:25:59,858 --> 00:26:02,160
Sam has been on a roll recently.

665
00:26:02,227 --> 00:26:04,529
He has won many stars
in his sweeping upturn.

666
00:26:04,596 --> 00:26:06,164
Some even say

667
00:26:06,231 --> 00:26:09,034
-he was nicknamed the SO Chef.
-SO Chef.

668
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:12,270
-Star-Obsessed Chef.
-Star-Obsessed Chef.

669
00:26:12,337 --> 00:26:14,406
-I didn't make it.
-SO Chef.

670
00:26:14,472 --> 00:26:16,074
He recently became scarier

671
00:26:16,141 --> 00:26:18,710
-because of his thirst for stars.
-True.

672
00:26:18,777 --> 00:26:20,445
-So I'm nervous.
-Right.

673
00:26:20,512 --> 00:26:23,148
He used to only make vegetable stock.

674
00:26:23,214 --> 00:26:25,984
BACK WHEN HE ALWAYS MADE VEGETABLE STOCK

675
00:26:26,051 --> 00:26:29,020
-Only vegetable stock.
-That's how he started out.

676
00:26:29,087 --> 00:26:31,022
Vegetable stock is great,

677
00:26:31,089 --> 00:26:32,557
but to win stars,

678
00:26:32,624 --> 00:26:34,259
I must abandon it from time to time.

679
00:26:34,326 --> 00:26:36,027
-He is the SO Chef.
-He is.

680
00:26:36,094 --> 00:26:37,796
-His canine is getting sharper.
-SO Chef.

681
00:26:37,862 --> 00:26:40,765
I really miss Sam's vegetable stock.

682
00:26:40,832 --> 00:26:42,133
When was the last time we've seen it?

683
00:26:42,200 --> 00:26:45,003
I'll make something similar

684
00:26:45,070 --> 00:26:47,072
-to vegetable stock.
-You will?

685
00:26:47,138 --> 00:26:49,107
-Will you make it?
-He must have a plan.

686
00:26:49,174 --> 00:26:52,077
Jong-won also says
he's confident about this round,

687
00:26:52,143 --> 00:26:54,045
saying he lives on eggs.

688
00:26:54,112 --> 00:26:55,914
-Is this true?
-It's true.

689
00:26:55,981 --> 00:26:58,383
I believe we began having eggs
in the green room

690
00:26:58,450 --> 00:27:00,085
ever since I joined the show.

691
00:27:00,151 --> 00:27:02,387
-That's true.
-We only had <i>ramyeon</i> before.

692
00:27:02,454 --> 00:27:05,323
But when I tried to eat some
before this round, they were all gone.

693
00:27:05,390 --> 00:27:07,225
-Someone else ate them.
-They're popular now.

694
00:27:07,292 --> 00:27:08,360
People add them to <i>ramyeon.</i>

695
00:27:08,426 --> 00:27:10,395
-Was it you?
-It wasn't me!

696
00:27:10,462 --> 00:27:12,664
I only had <i>ramyeon.</i>

697
00:27:12,731 --> 00:27:14,132
-Who was it?
-Who was it, Eun-yeong?

698
00:27:14,733 --> 00:27:16,134
TURNING HIMSELF IN

699
00:27:16,201 --> 00:27:18,236
-It was Ho-young.
-Chef Ho-young.

700
00:27:19,404 --> 00:27:22,107
I ate two and put one in my <i>ramyeon</i>.
I had three.

701
00:27:22,173 --> 00:27:23,475
You're so honest.

702
00:27:23,541 --> 00:27:25,110
I like eggs too.

703
00:27:25,410 --> 00:27:26,911
SNACKS ARE FIRST-COME, FIRST-SERVED

704
00:27:26,978 --> 00:27:30,682
-SO Chef, anything to say about eggs?
-"SO Chef"?

705
00:27:30,949 --> 00:27:32,717
NEW NICKNAME CONFIRMED

706
00:27:32,784 --> 00:27:36,087
J. Y. Park's fridge
is similar to my fridge.

707
00:27:36,154 --> 00:27:37,622
-Is it really?
-Yes.

708
00:27:37,689 --> 00:27:40,291
My son used to have atopic eczema.

709
00:27:40,358 --> 00:27:42,127
Really?

710
00:27:42,193 --> 00:27:44,329
Also, I always eat eggs in the morning,

711
00:27:44,396 --> 00:27:46,831
So it's an ingredient I can handle well.

712
00:27:47,666 --> 00:27:49,668
CHEF SAM'S INGREDIENTS:
EGG, BROWN CROAKER, ABALONE, PROSCIUTTO

713
00:27:49,734 --> 00:27:51,836
SAFFRON, KING OYSTER MUSHROOM,
SPINACH, ASPARAGUS, RADISH, AND MORE

714
00:27:51,903 --> 00:27:54,406
Chef Sam, what are you making?

715
00:27:54,472 --> 00:27:56,141
I'm going to make three dishes.

716
00:27:56,207 --> 00:27:58,043
First, I'll use a whole egg

717
00:27:58,109 --> 00:28:01,079
and make deviled eggs
with mayo from scratch,

718
00:28:01,146 --> 00:28:02,747
then I'll mix it with abalone

719
00:28:02,814 --> 00:28:05,216
and some asparagus.

720
00:28:05,283 --> 00:28:08,720
You'll be able to enjoy
a rich and savory whole egg dish.

721
00:28:08,787 --> 00:28:12,223
Second, I'll make a warm salad
with egg yolks,

722
00:28:12,290 --> 00:28:15,794
by stir-frying mushroom and spinach

723
00:28:15,860 --> 00:28:18,430
and covering it with hollandaise sauce.

724
00:28:18,496 --> 00:28:21,032
He had saffron, so I'll add a bit of that

725
00:28:21,099 --> 00:28:23,868
and make a salad
that brings out the egg yolk flavor.

726
00:28:23,935 --> 00:28:26,004
-Next is the main dish…
-Do you have time?

727
00:28:26,071 --> 00:28:27,505
…which uses egg whites.

728
00:28:27,572 --> 00:28:29,407
I'll make meringue,

729
00:28:29,474 --> 00:28:31,376
top it over the brown croaker,
and steam it together.

730
00:28:31,443 --> 00:28:33,445
So the three dishes will be

731
00:28:33,511 --> 00:28:34,946
a whole-egg dish,

732
00:28:35,013 --> 00:28:36,514
an egg yolk dish,

733
00:28:36,581 --> 00:28:39,484
and finally, an egg white dish.

734
00:28:39,551 --> 00:28:41,619
-It sounds like a course meal.
-That's crazy.

735
00:28:42,187 --> 00:28:44,422
Can he do that in 15 minutes?

736
00:28:45,256 --> 00:28:47,292
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
EGG, OCTOPUS, ABALONE, BEEF TENDERLOIN

737
00:28:47,358 --> 00:28:49,427
TOFU NOODLES, GREEK YOGURT,
WATER PARSLEY, RADISH SPROUTS, AND MORE

738
00:28:49,494 --> 00:28:51,629
What are you making, Jong-won?

739
00:28:51,696 --> 00:28:54,232
When J. Y. Park mentioned his peak,

740
00:28:54,299 --> 00:28:58,369
I thought every moment of his career
was a peak and thought it was admirable.

741
00:28:58,436 --> 00:29:00,972
I wanted to express my respect
for the peak of his career,

742
00:29:01,039 --> 00:29:05,376
-so I also prepared three egg dishes.
-You did?

743
00:29:05,443 --> 00:29:08,046
I'm making Korean food
to spread K-food around the world.

744
00:29:08,113 --> 00:29:09,481
-Korean food.
-I'll make a feast…

745
00:29:09,547 --> 00:29:12,117
-…for your <i>hwangap</i>, the 60th birthday.
-A feast?

746
00:29:12,183 --> 00:29:13,485
-His 60th birthday?
-But I thought

747
00:29:13,551 --> 00:29:16,020
a party would suit him better,
so I'll call it the 60th party.

748
00:29:16,087 --> 00:29:18,389
So I'll cook these for his 60th.

749
00:29:18,456 --> 00:29:21,326
-I'll begin with <i>suranchae.</i>
<i>-Suranchae</i>.

750
00:29:21,392 --> 00:29:23,928
It's a dish passed down through
the Gyeongju Choi family for generations.

751
00:29:23,995 --> 00:29:25,630
It's made with a poached egg,

752
00:29:25,697 --> 00:29:28,233
served with sesame seed juice.

753
00:29:28,299 --> 00:29:29,601
I'll also make <i>yukjeon</i>, beef <i>jeon</i>.

754
00:29:29,667 --> 00:29:32,437
-<i>Jeon</i> is a must for a feast, right?
-Beef <i>jeon</i> is delicious.

755
00:29:32,504 --> 00:29:35,940
Lastly, I'll prepare
soft steamed eggs with soy sauce,

756
00:29:36,007 --> 00:29:37,976
which will end your meal on a soft

757
00:29:38,042 --> 00:29:40,078
-and wet note.
-Soft and wet.

758
00:29:40,145 --> 00:29:43,248
Soft and wet. It'll be creamy.

759
00:29:43,314 --> 00:29:44,549
Deliciously.

760
00:29:44,616 --> 00:29:46,851
Cooking is usually the toughest part,

761
00:29:46,918 --> 00:29:48,887
but today, sharing the name was harder.

762
00:29:49,220 --> 00:29:50,455
TRYING HIS BEST

763
00:29:50,522 --> 00:29:53,091
This is how you grow.

764
00:29:53,158 --> 00:29:55,293
Are you sweaty? Why don't you wipe it off?

765
00:29:55,360 --> 00:29:57,195
Yes, I'm getting wet.

766
00:29:57,829 --> 00:29:59,030
He's ready to go.

767
00:29:59,097 --> 00:30:00,732
He's become a TV personality.

768
00:30:00,799 --> 00:30:03,168
It sounds like this will be a tough round.

769
00:30:03,234 --> 00:30:06,337
Now, let us begin the round,

770
00:30:06,404 --> 00:30:08,206
Peaking at 60.

771
00:30:08,273 --> 00:30:10,942
The Star-Obsessed Chef, aka SO Chef,

772
00:30:11,009 --> 00:30:12,877
Chef Sam Kim.

773
00:30:12,944 --> 00:30:15,613
Versus the Egg-Obsessed Chef,
aka EO Chef,

774
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:17,649
Chef Son Jong-won.

775
00:30:17,715 --> 00:30:19,317
-"EO Chef."
-Eggs and stars.

776
00:30:19,384 --> 00:30:21,986
Who'll help J. Y. Park stay at his peak?

777
00:30:22,053 --> 00:30:26,157
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

778
00:30:26,758 --> 00:30:27,792
JYP.

779
00:30:27,992 --> 00:30:28,827
LET'S DO THIS!

780
00:30:29,360 --> 00:30:30,562
EGGS

781
00:30:30,929 --> 00:30:32,730
SAM WILL MAKE THREE DIFFERENT EGG DISHES!

782
00:30:32,797 --> 00:30:33,798
It'll be a fierce round.

783
00:30:34,065 --> 00:30:35,667
HE BEGINS WITH THE BROWN CROAKER!

784
00:30:35,733 --> 00:30:37,569
They're using fish and meat, respectively.

785
00:30:37,635 --> 00:30:39,804
-Brown croaker.
-That's a meaty one.

786
00:30:39,871 --> 00:30:42,040
That's really…

787
00:30:42,106 --> 00:30:43,074
That fish is no joke.

788
00:30:43,141 --> 00:30:44,809
It's huge, isn't it?

789
00:30:44,876 --> 00:30:46,511
It's almost too big.

790
00:30:46,578 --> 00:30:47,712
-Wow.
-It's big.

791
00:30:48,646 --> 00:30:50,148
That brown croaker is insane.

792
00:30:50,748 --> 00:30:52,183
Look now plump the meat is.

793
00:30:52,684 --> 00:30:54,118
It looks so nice.

794
00:30:54,185 --> 00:30:56,054
Who'll finish prepping faster?

795
00:30:56,120 --> 00:30:57,989
He prepped the fish in a flash.

796
00:30:58,056 --> 00:31:00,692
-Fish. Fish stock.
-Are you adding the bones?

797
00:31:00,758 --> 00:31:02,560
-He's making fish bone broth.
-Bone broth.

798
00:31:02,627 --> 00:31:06,631
Brown croaker broth
turns out like beef bone broth.

799
00:31:06,698 --> 00:31:08,433
It's fish,
but it looks like beef bone broth.

800
00:31:08,499 --> 00:31:11,536
Brown croaker broth turns out milky-white,
and it's absolutely delicious.

801
00:31:11,603 --> 00:31:13,438
-Fish bone broth is really good.
-It's the best.

802
00:31:13,671 --> 00:31:15,139
A-HA

803
00:31:15,206 --> 00:31:18,476
That was why he said he'll make
something similar to vegetable stock.

804
00:31:18,543 --> 00:31:20,712
He's prepping the meat already.
It's for beef <i>jeon.</i>

805
00:31:20,778 --> 00:31:22,480
For beef <i>jeon,</i> it's very important

806
00:31:22,547 --> 00:31:25,683
-to make the slices even.
-I see.

807
00:31:26,384 --> 00:31:29,754
-Jong-won is making beef <i>jeon.</i>
-Those look perfect.

808
00:31:29,821 --> 00:31:32,423
I wanted to let J. Y. Park
sound even more tender,

809
00:31:32,490 --> 00:31:33,825
so I'll use tenderloin.

810
00:31:35,493 --> 00:31:37,362
JEEZ…

811
00:31:37,729 --> 00:31:38,663
WHAT WAS THAT?

812
00:31:38,730 --> 00:31:39,664
I'm disappointed.

813
00:31:39,731 --> 00:31:40,999
I'm disappointed.

814
00:31:41,065 --> 00:31:43,334
He's been dying to be funny lately.

815
00:31:46,804 --> 00:31:49,374
He's preparing the beef tenderloin
to make <i>jeon.</i>

816
00:31:49,440 --> 00:31:50,942
1. SALT AND PEPPER THINLY SLICED BEEF

817
00:31:51,009 --> 00:31:53,478
Jong-won seems to be taking the lead.

818
00:31:53,811 --> 00:31:55,179
2. STEAM ABALONE

819
00:31:55,246 --> 00:31:56,080
That's an abalone.

820
00:31:56,147 --> 00:31:59,317
-He's steaming an abalone.
-Steaming an abalone.

821
00:31:59,384 --> 00:32:01,352
-Meringue.
-Meringue.

822
00:32:01,419 --> 00:32:03,821
Sam grabs an egg.

823
00:32:04,622 --> 00:32:05,857
-Huh?
-Just the whites?

824
00:32:05,924 --> 00:32:07,358
-You got some yolk in there.
-Some got in.

825
00:32:07,425 --> 00:32:09,093
-It's fine.
-You can't use that.

826
00:32:09,160 --> 00:32:10,795
He can't make meringue with that.

827
00:32:10,862 --> 00:32:12,263
It's impossible to make meringue.

828
00:32:12,330 --> 00:32:13,364
Oh, no!

829
00:32:13,731 --> 00:32:14,632
OH, BOY

830
00:32:14,699 --> 00:32:16,367
It's an emergency.

831
00:32:16,567 --> 00:32:17,402
RETRY

832
00:32:17,468 --> 00:32:21,072
The organic egg!
The organic fertilized egg!

833
00:32:21,139 --> 00:32:22,607
We're sorry, chickens.

834
00:32:22,674 --> 00:32:26,778
J. Y. Park splurged his money
on nothing else but those eggs!

835
00:32:26,844 --> 00:32:28,880
-Gosh, what a waste.
-That's his everything!

836
00:32:29,247 --> 00:32:30,648
MAKING MERINGUE WITH NEW EGGS!

837
00:32:30,715 --> 00:32:33,818
Meringue.

838
00:32:34,018 --> 00:32:34,986
TAPPING

839
00:32:35,053 --> 00:32:36,955
What's Jong-won up to?

840
00:32:37,021 --> 00:32:38,723
I'm mixing eggs and yogurt.

841
00:32:38,790 --> 00:32:40,425
-Yogurt?
-Eggs and yogurt?

842
00:32:40,491 --> 00:32:41,659
He's adding syrup.

843
00:32:41,726 --> 00:32:43,628
3. MIX EGG, YOGURT, MAPLE SYRUP,
GRAIN SYRUP AND STRAIN

844
00:32:43,695 --> 00:32:45,897
He said he'd make steamed eggs,
so is that it?

845
00:32:47,131 --> 00:32:48,900
I'm trying to make "WET" egg pudding.

846
00:32:48,967 --> 00:32:50,568
"WET" egg pudding.

847
00:32:51,602 --> 00:32:55,006
He coated the insides of the bowls
with butter.

848
00:32:55,373 --> 00:32:57,575
POUR TO MAKE PUDDING-LIKE EGGS!

849
00:32:57,642 --> 00:32:59,344
You're making them like pudding.

850
00:32:59,410 --> 00:33:01,446
I guess he'll time them.

851
00:33:01,512 --> 00:33:02,613
He pulled out a timer.

852
00:33:02,680 --> 00:33:03,681
COVER

853
00:33:03,748 --> 00:33:05,116
He'll steam them like that?

854
00:33:05,183 --> 00:33:06,451
There's an abalone inside.

855
00:33:06,517 --> 00:33:07,785
He's going to steam them.

856
00:33:07,852 --> 00:33:10,555
4. BUTTER THE BOWLS,
POUR EGG MIXTURE, AND STEAM

857
00:33:10,888 --> 00:33:11,956
NEXT, POACHING AN EGG!

858
00:33:12,023 --> 00:33:14,392
-The poached egg.
-He'll poach an egg.

859
00:33:14,459 --> 00:33:16,427
-The poached egg.
-The poached egg.

860
00:33:16,494 --> 00:33:18,162
-The poached egg.
-The poached egg.

861
00:33:18,229 --> 00:33:20,498
The poached egg.
Swirl and poach the egg!

862
00:33:21,766 --> 00:33:23,835
YOU!

863
00:33:23,901 --> 00:33:25,336
He's unfazed.

864
00:33:25,403 --> 00:33:28,239
He's no longer the old Jong-won.

865
00:33:28,573 --> 00:33:29,907
SAM CUTS THE ORGANIC RADISH

866
00:33:30,141 --> 00:33:31,142
SLICED

867
00:33:31,209 --> 00:33:33,811
-What? What's he doing? A radish?
-Is that a radish?

868
00:33:33,878 --> 00:33:36,214
-He's grinding the radish.
-Radish.

869
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:38,549
Why are you grinding the radish? What for?

870
00:33:38,616 --> 00:33:40,351
Brown croaker.

871
00:33:40,418 --> 00:33:43,721
He topped the radish with brown croaker.

872
00:33:43,788 --> 00:33:45,590
-To steam them?
-He'll steam them together.

873
00:33:45,656 --> 00:33:46,858
That's how he'll steam it?

874
00:33:46,924 --> 00:33:48,059
I'll steam it.

875
00:33:48,126 --> 00:33:50,328
-To steam it? And meringue?
-The meringue is ready.

876
00:33:50,395 --> 00:33:51,396
Meringue? Why?

877
00:33:51,462 --> 00:33:53,464
-Will he put it on top?
-He said he would.

878
00:33:53,531 --> 00:33:54,665
-On top.
-And steam it.

879
00:33:54,732 --> 00:33:58,669
He mixed ground radish with the meringue.

880
00:33:58,736 --> 00:33:59,837
Steamed brown croaker with meringue.

881
00:33:59,904 --> 00:34:00,838
GASP

882
00:34:00,905 --> 00:34:02,907
That's how you do it? That's new.

883
00:34:02,974 --> 00:34:05,309
-Will it get cooked in time?
-Why are you using radish?

884
00:34:05,376 --> 00:34:07,578
-As the support.
-The support.

885
00:34:07,645 --> 00:34:09,313
He doesn't have to eat it, then?

886
00:34:09,680 --> 00:34:12,950
NEXT, THE EGG YOLK DISH!
MINCED GARLIC

887
00:34:14,585 --> 00:34:16,854
He's adding lemon juice.

888
00:34:16,921 --> 00:34:17,989
That lemon.

889
00:34:18,056 --> 00:34:20,258
It's really hard to find organic lemons,

890
00:34:20,324 --> 00:34:24,028
so whenever they're available,
I rake in as many as I can find.

891
00:34:24,095 --> 00:34:26,297
Then I wash them
with organic sanitizing solution,

892
00:34:26,364 --> 00:34:30,268
wrap them in kitchen towels,
and seal them up in a zipper bag.

893
00:34:31,069 --> 00:34:34,038
Those lemons smell
much stronger than you'd expect.

894
00:34:34,105 --> 00:34:35,306
He says it smells strong.

895
00:34:35,373 --> 00:34:37,275
4. MAKE LEMON MAYO
WITH EGG YOLK AND CONDIMENTS

896
00:34:37,341 --> 00:34:38,309
Ten minutes left.

897
00:34:38,376 --> 00:34:40,378
It's been five minutes already.

898
00:34:40,445 --> 00:34:41,279
IN!

899
00:34:41,479 --> 00:34:42,847
CRUNCHY WATER PARSLEY STEMS!

900
00:34:42,914 --> 00:34:44,882
What's that? Long-legged octopus?

901
00:34:44,949 --> 00:34:46,317
Octopus? It looks like it.

902
00:34:46,384 --> 00:34:47,819
-Octopus?
-Octopuses are always a must

903
00:34:47,885 --> 00:34:50,221
for a feast at a nobleman's house.

904
00:34:50,455 --> 00:34:51,422
THAT'S TRUE

905
00:34:51,489 --> 00:34:54,826
So instead of an octopus,
I'll use the long-legged one.

906
00:34:54,892 --> 00:34:56,327
Isn't it too early to have his 60th feast?

907
00:34:56,394 --> 00:34:57,595
6. BLANCH LONG-LEGGED OCTOPUS LEGS

908
00:34:57,662 --> 00:35:00,131
His 60th party.

909
00:35:00,198 --> 00:35:01,265
His 60th party.

910
00:35:01,332 --> 00:35:02,366
DONE

911
00:35:02,733 --> 00:35:04,836
PLACE THE BLANCHED WATER PARSLEY
AND OCTOPUS IN ICED WATER!

912
00:35:05,369 --> 00:35:07,205
TIMER FOR THE POACHED EGG!

913
00:35:07,905 --> 00:35:09,907
He's very methodical.

914
00:35:09,974 --> 00:35:12,543
-That looks great.
-The egg got poached perfectly.

915
00:35:12,610 --> 00:35:15,446
-It's as plump as a baby's face.
-The egg got poached.

916
00:35:15,513 --> 00:35:18,382
It looks similar to burrata,
his favorite cheese.

917
00:35:18,449 --> 00:35:19,617
You're right.

918
00:35:19,684 --> 00:35:22,453
He's getting the abalone.

919
00:35:22,520 --> 00:35:23,921
That abalone is nice and plump.

920
00:35:23,988 --> 00:35:27,225
He's using all those for <i>suranchae.</i>

921
00:35:27,291 --> 00:35:29,127
You have around eight minutes left,

922
00:35:29,193 --> 00:35:31,195
about halfway done.

923
00:35:32,196 --> 00:35:33,464
-King oyster mushroom.
-King oyster mushroom.

924
00:35:35,933 --> 00:35:37,935
-You don't have much time.
-Organic mushroom.

925
00:35:38,269 --> 00:35:40,404
5. COOK KING OYSTER MUSHROOM
IN GARLIC BUTTER

926
00:35:40,705 --> 00:35:42,707
INGREDIENT PREPPING:
1. KING OYSTER MUSHROOM

927
00:35:43,508 --> 00:35:45,843
Sam cooks spinach.

928
00:35:45,910 --> 00:35:48,179
I have a feeling
that he might run out of time.

929
00:35:48,246 --> 00:35:49,547
You have to make hollandaise too.

930
00:35:49,614 --> 00:35:50,882
He has to make the sauce too.

931
00:35:51,249 --> 00:35:53,751
HOLLANDAISE SAUCE MADE WITH EGG YOLKS!

932
00:35:54,018 --> 00:35:55,586
7. STIR EGG YOLK OVER LOW HEAT

933
00:35:55,653 --> 00:35:56,954
Huh?

934
00:35:57,021 --> 00:35:58,923
You have to keep stirring it.

935
00:35:59,357 --> 00:36:00,258
THERE'S SO MUCH TO DO…

936
00:36:00,324 --> 00:36:02,493
He could use an extra pair of hands.

937
00:36:03,327 --> 00:36:04,629
SWITCHING AGAIN

938
00:36:05,029 --> 00:36:06,397
SPINACH

939
00:36:06,464 --> 00:36:08,466
-Spinach?
-Spinach is done.

940
00:36:08,533 --> 00:36:09,567
PREPPING: 2. SPINACH

941
00:36:10,334 --> 00:36:11,369
ASPARAGUS

942
00:36:11,435 --> 00:36:12,270
BUTTER

943
00:36:12,537 --> 00:36:14,972
8. COOK CHOPPED ASPARAGUS IN GARLIC BUTTER

944
00:36:15,540 --> 00:36:17,141
He said he'd make a warm salad.

945
00:36:17,208 --> 00:36:19,243
-You have seven minutes left.
-Seven minutes.

946
00:36:19,310 --> 00:36:20,511
When will he make beef <i>jeon?</i>

947
00:36:20,845 --> 00:36:23,314
JONG-WON HAS A LONG WAY TO GO TOO!

948
00:36:23,548 --> 00:36:24,649
A WHOLE EGG

949
00:36:24,715 --> 00:36:26,551
QUICKLY PREPARING THE EGG WASH!

950
00:36:26,617 --> 00:36:27,685
What's that for?

951
00:36:27,752 --> 00:36:28,853
-Beef <i>jeon.</i>
-Beef <i>jeon.</i>

952
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:31,022
-For beef <i>jeon.</i>
-Yes, beef <i>jeon.</i>

953
00:36:31,088 --> 00:36:32,757
J. Y. Park knows more

954
00:36:32,823 --> 00:36:34,959
than Hyun-suk.

955
00:36:35,026 --> 00:36:37,461
The two chefs

956
00:36:37,528 --> 00:36:39,664
are extra-focused today.

957
00:36:39,730 --> 00:36:41,599
This is no joke.

958
00:36:42,200 --> 00:36:44,101
They're serious.

959
00:36:44,168 --> 00:36:47,405
Both chefs have to make three dishes.

960
00:36:47,471 --> 00:36:49,540
They have to make all three.

961
00:36:49,607 --> 00:36:51,976
So that's the dish
the Gyeongju Choi family eats.

962
00:36:52,043 --> 00:36:54,879
It's a well-established dish.

963
00:36:54,946 --> 00:36:56,013
7. MAKE SESAME JUICE

964
00:36:56,080 --> 00:36:57,882
What's that? Balsamic vinegar?

965
00:36:57,949 --> 00:37:00,084
He's using organic maple syrup.

966
00:37:00,151 --> 00:37:01,719
8. MAKE MAPLE SOY SAUCE GLAZE

967
00:37:03,454 --> 00:37:04,288
GARLIC

968
00:37:04,355 --> 00:37:05,423
There's no time.

969
00:37:06,257 --> 00:37:08,392
PROSCIUTTO

970
00:37:08,459 --> 00:37:10,127
I think he used vinegar.

971
00:37:10,194 --> 00:37:11,896
OCCASIONALLY WORKS ON
THE HOLLANDAISE SAUCE…

972
00:37:11,963 --> 00:37:13,898
BOILED EGGS

973
00:37:14,865 --> 00:37:15,766
PROSCIUTTO AND FISH BROTH!

974
00:37:15,833 --> 00:37:18,603
-He's mixing something.
-It's the brown croaker broth.

975
00:37:18,669 --> 00:37:20,805
Just the fish broth, no seasoning.

976
00:37:20,871 --> 00:37:22,440
Brown croaker, garlic, and ham.

977
00:37:22,506 --> 00:37:24,809
That hollandaise sauce is the problem.

978
00:37:24,875 --> 00:37:27,578
-Hollandaise.
-It hasn't turned into cream.

979
00:37:27,645 --> 00:37:30,414
-I don't think he added butter.
-I haven't yet.

980
00:37:30,481 --> 00:37:31,482
You didn't, right?

981
00:37:31,549 --> 00:37:32,950
What's he adding?

982
00:37:33,017 --> 00:37:34,986
Melted butter.

983
00:37:35,052 --> 00:37:37,188
Only five minutes left.

984
00:37:37,255 --> 00:37:38,823
It's almost five minutes.

985
00:37:38,889 --> 00:37:40,758
But they both haven't made a single dish.

986
00:37:40,825 --> 00:37:43,794
Right? They should've finished one by now.

987
00:37:43,861 --> 00:37:45,896
-Can they do this?
-No matter how busy you are…

988
00:37:46,697 --> 00:37:47,565
Abalone?

989
00:37:47,632 --> 00:37:49,634
-Abalone.
-Abalone.

990
00:37:49,700 --> 00:37:50,935
