to see the person behind the dish,

1233
00:47:01,752 --> 00:47:04,321
and I can see it completely.

1234
00:47:04,388 --> 00:47:05,455
I love that for this dish.

1235
00:47:05,522 --> 00:47:08,292
-You can see Sam in it?
-It's that classy?

1236
00:47:08,358 --> 00:47:12,462
His face, gestures,
and expressions are all in here.

1237
00:47:12,529 --> 00:47:13,564
I love it.

1238
00:47:13,630 --> 00:47:15,532
THE DISH IS LIKE HIS OTHER SELF

1239
00:47:15,599 --> 00:47:17,968
And there's a bit more bass than before.

1240
00:47:18,035 --> 00:47:19,937
But I still want
to raise the EQ a little more.

1241
00:47:20,404 --> 00:47:21,705
GOSH…

1242
00:47:21,772 --> 00:47:24,274
-Why are you so excited?
-He still needed more.

1243
00:47:24,341 --> 00:47:27,311
That one is the brown croaker,
cooked with meringue.

1244
00:47:27,377 --> 00:47:31,081
Try the meringue, the fish,
and the broth altogether.

1245
00:47:31,148 --> 00:47:34,184
-It's made with fish bones, right?
-Yes, I made it with fish bones.

1246
00:47:34,251 --> 00:47:36,687
-This one must have the bass.
-Bass.

1247
00:47:36,753 --> 00:47:38,889
-The bass.
-It has to be here.

1248
00:47:38,956 --> 00:47:42,426
-Ready to dance?
-Will he hit him with the bass at the end?

1249
00:47:42,492 --> 00:47:44,928
Was that why the first two were weaker?

1250
00:47:44,995 --> 00:47:45,863
SCOOPS A BIG BITE

1251
00:47:48,365 --> 00:47:49,900
WILL HE FIND THE BASS?

1252
00:47:49,967 --> 00:47:52,469
-The bass.
-Thump.

1253
00:47:52,536 --> 00:47:53,971
You're acting up a lot today.

1254
00:47:56,173 --> 00:47:58,475
DOESN'T CARE AND CONTINUES TO PLAY

1255
00:47:58,542 --> 00:48:00,043
Is the fish cooked well?

1256
00:48:00,110 --> 00:48:02,779
Gosh, it's perfect. The doneness…

1257
00:48:02,846 --> 00:48:06,950
The quality is just unbelievable,
and I'm into these things.

1258
00:48:07,017 --> 00:48:10,454
He doesn't compromise
the flavor of the ingredients.

1259
00:48:10,520 --> 00:48:13,790
I can taste the fish, the mushroom,
and everything on its own.

1260
00:48:13,857 --> 00:48:16,193
It's very sophisticated,
and really delicious.

1261
00:48:16,260 --> 00:48:18,996
-Right?
-That's Sam's style too.

1262
00:48:19,062 --> 00:48:22,866
He's not into dishes
that are too strong or deep.

1263
00:48:22,933 --> 00:48:25,369
This is taking the concept of balance

1264
00:48:25,435 --> 00:48:26,803
to its pinnacle.

1265
00:48:26,870 --> 00:48:29,106
It shows what balance is.

1266
00:48:29,172 --> 00:48:32,876
He had a brown croaker,
so he picked out the ingredients

1267
00:48:32,943 --> 00:48:35,012
that will not go against the dish.

1268
00:48:35,078 --> 00:48:36,914
THE STEAMED FISH HAS NO DOWNSIDE

1269
00:48:36,980 --> 00:48:39,783
-Next, Chef Jong-won's meal.
-Jong-won's meal.

1270
00:48:41,385 --> 00:48:43,287
BEEF JEON

1271
00:48:44,621 --> 00:48:47,324
SURANCHAE

1272
00:48:47,991 --> 00:48:49,626
STEAMED EGG

1273
00:48:50,427 --> 00:48:51,461
CHEF SON JONG-WON'S DON'T LEAVE ME, EGG

1274
00:48:51,528 --> 00:48:53,597
First, break the poached egg.

1275
00:48:53,664 --> 00:48:57,935
I made sesame juice
by blending sesame seeds with farro milk.

1276
00:48:58,001 --> 00:49:02,439
-Farro.
-A meal for Nobleman Park's 60th birthday.

1277
00:49:02,940 --> 00:49:07,511
It's a dish that originated
from the Gyeongju Choi family.

1278
00:49:07,577 --> 00:49:11,148
So I wanted to serve it at the feast
for the leader of JYP Entertainment.

1279
00:49:11,214 --> 00:49:12,716
It's called <i>suranchae.</i>

1280
00:49:12,783 --> 00:49:15,285
Poached egg with some seafood.

1281
00:49:15,352 --> 00:49:16,853
A BIG BITE

1282
00:49:16,920 --> 00:49:19,022
-I wonder what it's like.
-What's his dish like?

1283
00:49:21,058 --> 00:49:22,526
CURIOUS

1284
00:49:23,293 --> 00:49:24,861
Sam's dish just now

1285
00:49:24,928 --> 00:49:29,399
felt like the sound was evenly spread
from the mid-range to the high range.

1286
00:49:30,167 --> 00:49:32,869
But in this, the poached egg
comes through clearly in the mid-range.

1287
00:49:32,936 --> 00:49:35,405
It hits you in the middle.

1288
00:49:35,472 --> 00:49:38,375
It definitely brings out
the savory egg flavor.

1289
00:49:39,476 --> 00:49:42,646
This poached egg does have a hold on me.

1290
00:49:42,713 --> 00:49:45,983
It would've been great
if the sauce had a bit more low-mid range.

1291
00:49:46,049 --> 00:49:47,284
Actually,

1292
00:49:47,351 --> 00:49:50,354
I lowered the EQ
and made the appetizer bland on purpose.

1293
00:49:50,687 --> 00:49:52,723
IT WAS INTENTIONAL

1294
00:49:52,789 --> 00:49:54,891
You made an acoustic track.

1295
00:49:54,958 --> 00:49:58,462
-Unplugged. It's an unplugged dish.
-Unplugged!

1296
00:49:59,029 --> 00:50:00,497
JOINING HIS SHENANIGANS

1297
00:50:01,398 --> 00:50:02,933
What's next?

1298
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:06,403
Beef <i>jeon.</i> And eggs are a must
when you make <i>jeon.</i>

1299
00:50:06,470 --> 00:50:08,905
Also, it's usually served
with seasoned green onion,

1300
00:50:08,972 --> 00:50:12,109
but instead, I topped it
with lightly seasoned radish sprouts.

1301
00:50:12,175 --> 00:50:16,446
The important thing is for the egg
to hit him with the savory flavor.

1302
00:50:16,513 --> 00:50:17,647
PICKS UP THE WHOLE THING

1303
00:50:17,714 --> 00:50:20,484
AND INTO HIS MOUTH

1304
00:50:20,550 --> 00:50:21,918
He even eats like an expert.

1305
00:50:21,985 --> 00:50:24,221
AN EXPERT

1306
00:50:24,287 --> 00:50:25,489
And he's savoring it.

1307
00:50:26,056 --> 00:50:27,691
I believe really good cooks

1308
00:50:27,758 --> 00:50:29,993
are the ones who make
great dishes with tenderloin.

1309
00:50:30,060 --> 00:50:31,828
The seasoning is perfect.

1310
00:50:31,895 --> 00:50:33,663
It must be seasoned perfectly.

1311
00:50:33,730 --> 00:50:35,132
Does it hit you with some bass?

1312
00:50:35,198 --> 00:50:36,433
-Yes.
-It's the seasoning.

1313
00:50:36,500 --> 00:50:39,036
-It was the seasoning.
-The salt.

1314
00:50:39,102 --> 00:50:41,104
-He needed salt.
-It was salt.

1315
00:50:41,171 --> 00:50:44,007
Salt brings out the flavor
of the dish itself.

1316
00:50:44,074 --> 00:50:45,742
-Yes.
-It doesn't just make it saltier.

1317
00:50:45,809 --> 00:50:47,377
The bass of cooking

1318
00:50:47,444 --> 00:50:49,546
-is salt.
-That's why.

1319
00:50:49,613 --> 00:50:53,083
That's why you keep salting your food,
even when you don't have time.

1320
00:50:53,683 --> 00:50:55,419
THE EXPERT OF SALT

1321
00:50:55,485 --> 00:50:57,254
The thickness of the tenderloin

1322
00:50:57,320 --> 00:50:59,056
is perfection.

1323
00:50:59,122 --> 00:51:00,857
-It must be perfect.
-Is it even?

1324
00:51:00,924 --> 00:51:04,795
<i>It would've been worse</i>
<i>if it were any thicker or thinner.</i>

1325
00:51:04,861 --> 00:51:06,029
<i>It seemed very important.</i>

1326
00:51:06,096 --> 00:51:07,364
-What's this?
-Use the small spoon.

1327
00:51:07,431 --> 00:51:09,032
-The small spoon.
-It's on the side.

1328
00:51:09,099 --> 00:51:11,101
-Steamed egg.
-I mixed yogurt and egg,

1329
00:51:11,168 --> 00:51:13,603
<i>and topped it with a mix</i>
<i>of maple syrup and soy sauce.</i>

1330
00:51:13,670 --> 00:51:16,239
-It was organic maple syrup too.
-A "WET" dish.

1331
00:51:16,306 --> 00:51:18,341
-It's creamy and "WET."
-It's Japanese-style, right?

1332
00:51:18,408 --> 00:51:20,644
It'll feel like pudding.

1333
00:51:20,710 --> 00:51:22,079
-Like pudding.
-Caramel pudding.

1334
00:51:22,145 --> 00:51:24,314
Steamed eggs in Japan
remind me of pudding.

1335
00:51:25,449 --> 00:51:26,283
AMAZED…

1336
00:51:26,349 --> 00:51:29,052
DEEPLY IN AWE

1337
00:51:29,119 --> 00:51:32,389
That was almost half air, half eggs.

1338
00:51:32,456 --> 00:51:33,790
Right?

1339
00:51:33,857 --> 00:51:35,425
-The sweetness.
-It was the sweetness.

1340
00:51:35,492 --> 00:51:37,227
-The sweetness.
-It was the sweetness.

1341
00:51:37,294 --> 00:51:38,595
It was the sweetness.

1342
00:51:38,662 --> 00:51:41,565
HE LIKES HIS FOOD SWEET AND SALTY…

1343
00:51:41,631 --> 00:51:44,201
-And saltiness.
-He has a sweet tooth!

1344
00:51:44,267 --> 00:51:46,603
-My gosh.
-What about last week?

1345
00:51:46,670 --> 00:51:48,672
-He's been holding himself back.
-It the sweetness?

1346
00:51:48,738 --> 00:51:50,607
It almost doesn't mix well.

1347
00:51:50,674 --> 00:51:55,412
The syrup, the soy sauce,
and the savoriness of the egg don't mix,

1348
00:51:55,479 --> 00:51:57,247
but there's a harmony.

1349
00:51:57,314 --> 00:51:58,682
A harmony within incongruity.

1350
00:51:58,748 --> 00:52:01,885
Even in music,
dissonance can sometimes sound cooler.

1351
00:52:01,952 --> 00:52:03,453
You're right.

1352
00:52:03,520 --> 00:52:05,655
Chef Hyun-suk brings up music now.

1353
00:52:05,722 --> 00:52:09,059
This is why he sings wedding songs!

1354
00:52:09,126 --> 00:52:11,728
He's an expert wedding singer.

1355
00:52:11,795 --> 00:52:15,398
You should sing a song
for his 60th party too!

1356
00:52:15,465 --> 00:52:17,000
PLAYING MUSIC HIMSELF

1357
00:52:17,067 --> 00:52:19,769
You're going to get punched.

1358
00:52:20,704 --> 00:52:22,405
-He'll punch you.
-Go on, and I'll punch you.

1359
00:52:24,074 --> 00:52:26,810
It's time to find out
the decision J. Y. Park made.

1360
00:52:26,877 --> 00:52:28,345
<i>Press one for Son Jong-won</i>

1361
00:52:29,045 --> 00:52:31,081
<i>and two for Sam Kim.</i>

1362
00:52:31,982 --> 00:52:33,850
-It's tough.
-This will be fun.

1363
00:52:33,917 --> 00:52:36,653
Please make your decision
and push the button!

1364
00:52:39,589 --> 00:52:41,124
He pushed one.

1365
00:52:41,191 --> 00:52:43,260
-The legends choose quickly.
-They do.

1366
00:52:43,326 --> 00:52:47,130
It's because they must
always decide quickly.

1367
00:52:47,197 --> 00:52:49,065
Let's find out the result.

1368
00:52:49,132 --> 00:52:50,500
Show us!

1369
00:52:50,567 --> 00:52:53,236
They had to make egg dishes
to help him peak.

1370
00:52:53,303 --> 00:52:56,540
Will Sam, who's looking to
get revenge on Jong-won,

1371
00:52:56,606 --> 00:52:58,375
take his victory?

1372
00:52:58,441 --> 00:53:00,410
Or will Jong-won defeat Sam

1373
00:53:00,477 --> 00:53:02,913
and finally reach his tenth victory?

1374
00:53:02,979 --> 00:53:05,849
Who did J. Y. Park choose?

1375
00:53:06,383 --> 00:53:08,652
-A dish to let him peak at 60!
-Which one is it?

1376
00:53:09,186 --> 00:53:10,353
The egg dishes!

1377
00:53:11,254 --> 00:53:12,455
SON JONG-WON WINS

1378
00:53:12,522 --> 00:53:13,990
It was Jong-won.

1379
00:53:14,291 --> 00:53:16,826
CHEF SON JONG-WON'S
DON'T LEAVE ME, EGG WINS

1380
00:53:17,394 --> 00:53:18,995
-Great work.
-It was Jong-won.

1381
00:53:20,597 --> 00:53:23,567
Chef Jong-won was chosen.

1382
00:53:23,633 --> 00:53:25,468
Both were immensely high in quality,

1383
00:53:25,535 --> 00:53:28,371
but Chef Jong-won was bolder.

1384
00:53:28,438 --> 00:53:33,043
The difference was that he chose
to have more ups and downs.

1385
00:53:33,109 --> 00:53:36,179
-He finally reaches two digits
-Two digits.

1386
00:53:36,246 --> 00:53:38,281
-with ten wins.
-Ten wins.

1387
00:53:38,348 --> 00:53:40,317
-Let's hear from him.
-Actually,

1388
00:53:40,383 --> 00:53:43,587
"Don't Leave Me" was released in 1994,

1389
00:53:43,653 --> 00:53:46,590
and I was often comforted
while listening to that song.

1390
00:53:46,656 --> 00:53:48,225
I was in fourth grade.

1391
00:53:48,291 --> 00:53:50,393
-Comforted?
-A kid needed to be comforted?

1392
00:53:50,460 --> 00:53:52,629
-He'd get scolded by his mom.
-School's tough too.

1393
00:53:52,696 --> 00:53:54,030
Life is tough at that age.

1394
00:53:54,864 --> 00:53:55,765
And studying with a tutor.

1395
00:53:55,832 --> 00:53:58,535
The 11-year-old Jong-won
was often comforted by his song.

1396
00:53:58,602 --> 00:54:02,105
It was such an honor
to sing this dish's name before you.

1397
00:54:02,939 --> 00:54:06,042
Please put a star on Chef Jong-won!

1398
00:54:06,109 --> 00:54:07,611
-Great job.
-Great job.

1399
00:54:08,345 --> 00:54:09,913
It goes right above his name tag.

1400
00:54:09,980 --> 00:54:12,682
This is Jong-won's tenth star,
making him the sole first place!

1401
00:54:12,749 --> 00:54:14,417
JONG-WON TAKES HIS 10TH STAR

1402
00:54:14,484 --> 00:54:16,219
Jong-won becomes the sole first place.

1403
00:54:17,053 --> 00:54:21,291
<i>This is the second round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with J. Y. Park's ingredients.</i>

1404
00:54:21,358 --> 00:54:22,692
SUF.

1405
00:54:22,759 --> 00:54:25,729
Street Unorthodox Fighter.

1406
00:54:25,795 --> 00:54:26,863
HIGH EXPECTATIONS

1407
00:54:26,930 --> 00:54:29,799
The meat expert
who trained in a plastic greenhouse,

1408
00:54:29,866 --> 00:54:32,068
Director Yoo Yong-wook,

1409
00:54:32,135 --> 00:54:34,938
is against the man who's been
picking up skills from chefs for 13 years,

1410
00:54:35,005 --> 00:54:38,775
the master freeloader,
Webtoon Artist Kim Poong.

1411
00:54:38,842 --> 00:54:43,380
Director Yong-wook finally stands
in the <i>Chef & My Fridge</i> kitchen.

1412
00:54:43,446 --> 00:54:44,547
How do you feel?

1413
00:54:44,614 --> 00:54:46,016
I'm worried,

1414
00:54:46,082 --> 00:54:47,651
not sure if I'll do a good job,

1415
00:54:47,717 --> 00:54:50,654
and I don't know if going against Poong
was the right choice.

1416
00:54:50,720 --> 00:54:51,588
My head is full.

1417
00:54:51,955 --> 00:54:55,091
WELCOME TO THE KIM POONG ERA

1418
00:54:55,158 --> 00:54:58,395
-I saw you using grills on <i>CCW2.</i>
-Yes.

1419
00:54:58,461 --> 00:54:59,763
Did you bring one today?

1420
00:54:59,829 --> 00:55:01,698
I brought a special one, actually,

1421
00:55:01,765 --> 00:55:04,200
to show how determined I am.

1422
00:55:04,267 --> 00:55:07,837
-Is there a story?
-It's my first grill, bought back in 1995.

1423
00:55:07,904 --> 00:55:10,373
-It's the same age as me.
-Your very first grill?

1424
00:55:10,440 --> 00:55:11,808
You bought that in 1995?

1425
00:55:11,875 --> 00:55:14,377
I was in third grade,
so it was my dad, not me.

1426
00:55:14,444 --> 00:55:16,680
-I was ten years old back then.
-You were ten.

1427
00:55:16,746 --> 00:55:19,749
-It's so special.
-The handles used to be made of wood.

1428
00:55:19,816 --> 00:55:20,884
I can see that.

1429
00:55:20,950 --> 00:55:24,821
The top handle came off,
so I had it fixed with metal.

1430
00:55:24,888 --> 00:55:28,892
-That's cool.
-I made my first grilled meat using this.

1431
00:55:28,958 --> 00:55:31,895
-Story is very important.
-Of course, it is.

1432
00:55:31,961 --> 00:55:34,164
YONG-WOOK TAKES THE LEAD WITH HIS STORY

1433
00:55:34,230 --> 00:55:35,465
I also brought

1434
00:55:35,532 --> 00:55:37,067
something my father made,

1435
00:55:37,133 --> 00:55:38,301
my body.

1436
00:55:38,368 --> 00:55:40,470
So I'll do my best too.

1437
00:55:40,537 --> 00:55:42,372
-That's everyone's case.
-He's not lying.

1438
00:55:42,439 --> 00:55:45,975
The body my dad has made is different.

1439
00:55:46,042 --> 00:55:48,912
Don't be so cheeky
and talk when it's your turn.

1440
00:55:48,978 --> 00:55:50,613
It's my first time

1441
00:55:50,680 --> 00:55:54,050
seeing him at a shoot
for <i>Chef & My Fridge,</i>

1442
00:55:54,117 --> 00:55:55,952
and he acts like
he's getting hunted down.

1443
00:55:57,087 --> 00:56:00,090
-You caught on to that.
-He's too rushed.

1444
00:56:00,156 --> 00:56:03,126
It's as if he's being hunted down
by the laughter hunter.

1445
00:56:03,193 --> 00:56:05,061
I'm seeing his new side today.

1446
00:56:06,329 --> 00:56:08,264
DIRECTOR YONG-WOOK'S INGREDIENTS:
PORK BELLY, PORK NECK, KIMCHI

1447
00:56:08,331 --> 00:56:10,500
TOFU NOODLES, SESAME, PEANUT,
CASHEW NUT SPREAD, MAPLE SUGAR, AND MORE

1448
00:56:10,567 --> 00:56:12,769
Director Yong-wook,
tell us about your dish.

1449
00:56:13,503 --> 00:56:16,606
The dish I'll be making is

1450
00:56:17,807 --> 00:56:20,076
-SeYP.
-SeYP?

1451
00:56:20,143 --> 00:56:22,445
-Like JYP?
-JYP?

1452
00:56:22,512 --> 00:56:25,448
-Not JYP, but SeYP?
-SeYP.

1453
00:56:25,515 --> 00:56:29,619
The way I cook requires fire and smoke,

1454
00:56:29,686 --> 00:56:34,858
but J. Y. Park's fridge didn't have
anything I could make smoke with.

1455
00:56:34,924 --> 00:56:36,826
I will use charcoal and whatnot,

1456
00:56:36,893 --> 00:56:38,728
but I did find organic sesame seeds.

1457
00:56:38,795 --> 00:56:40,897
So I'll use that
and add an aroma of sesame.

1458
00:56:41,331 --> 00:56:42,165
AMAZED

1459
00:56:42,232 --> 00:56:43,800
You said

1460
00:56:43,867 --> 00:56:47,637
you'd go abroad to spread
K-culture and K-pop,

1461
00:56:47,704 --> 00:56:50,774
and you always had
kimchi and pork when you returned.

1462
00:56:50,840 --> 00:56:54,010
I wanted to cook pork
and let you wrap it with kimchi,

1463
00:56:54,077 --> 00:56:55,879
just like how I thought
I should embrace you.

1464
00:56:55,945 --> 00:56:58,415
-By wrapping?
-By wrapping it around.

1465
00:56:58,481 --> 00:57:00,517
-That story.
-Embrace him in the arms of pork.

1466
00:57:00,583 --> 00:57:05,054
I'll create smoke with sesame seeds
and embrace him with that first.

1467
00:57:05,121 --> 00:57:07,323
I would say sesame seeds
have the Korean aroma.

1468
00:57:07,390 --> 00:57:08,825
TOUCHED

1469
00:57:08,892 --> 00:57:10,460
It'll be a savory hug.

1470
00:57:10,527 --> 00:57:12,495
-Yes, right?
-Savory and nutty.

1471
00:57:13,329 --> 00:57:15,298
WEBTOON ARTIST POONG'S INGREDIENTS:
PORK BELLY, PORK NECK, KIMCHI

1472
00:57:15,365 --> 00:57:17,500
TTEOKBOKKI TTEOK, POTATO, MAPLE SYRUP,
GRAIN SYRUP, KETCHUP, SESAME OIL

1473
00:57:17,567 --> 00:57:18,935
As a long-time fan of J. Y. Park,

1474
00:57:19,002 --> 00:57:24,340
when I saw his pink plastic pants
from last year's WATERBOMB Festival,

1475
00:57:24,407 --> 00:57:26,309
I was deeply inspired.

1476
00:57:26,376 --> 00:57:28,344
I will use food ingredients

1477
00:57:28,411 --> 00:57:31,181
to make plastic pants.

1478
00:57:31,247 --> 00:57:32,582
Plastic pants? How?

1479
00:57:32,649 --> 00:57:34,584
Plastic pants with food?

1480
00:57:34,651 --> 00:57:35,685
Can he eat them?

1481
00:57:35,752 --> 00:57:37,487
-He can.
-He can eat them.

1482
00:57:38,321 --> 00:57:39,656
Gosh, this is…

1483
00:57:39,722 --> 00:57:41,658
-Yes.
-They'll be organic plastic pants.

1484
00:57:41,724 --> 00:57:45,462
-It'll be story versus theme.
-Story versus theme.

1485
00:57:45,528 --> 00:57:49,165
Also, you had
your favorite food, rice cake,

1486
00:57:49,232 --> 00:57:51,134
so I'll do whatever it takes
to use it somehow.

1487
00:57:51,401 --> 00:57:53,369
HAPPY RICE CAKE GORILLA

1488
00:57:53,436 --> 00:57:55,738
-And now,
-Whoa. We're starting now?

1489
00:57:55,805 --> 00:57:58,875
…let us begin
the Half Pork, Half Kimchi round

1490
00:57:58,942 --> 00:58:00,510
-for J. Y. Park.
-Stay calm.

1491
00:58:00,577 --> 00:58:01,678
Lights, please.

1492
00:58:01,744 --> 00:58:03,213
Timer, please.

1493
00:58:03,279 --> 00:58:04,113
HALF PORK, HALF KIMCHI ROUND BEGINS!

1494
00:58:04,347 --> 00:58:05,415
THE GATEKEEPER AWAKENS

1495
00:58:06,082 --> 00:58:06,916
My gosh!

1496
00:58:06,983 --> 00:58:09,152
-You got this!
-It won't be easy.

1497
00:58:09,219 --> 00:58:10,487
ARGH!

1498
00:58:10,553 --> 00:58:12,322
I will turn you into a lump of charcoal!

1499
00:58:12,388 --> 00:58:14,624
Okay. I got this.

1500
00:58:14,691 --> 00:58:18,528
Here to gift J. Y. Park
the best plastic pants of his life,

1501
00:58:18,595 --> 00:58:20,230
Webtoon Artist Kim Poong.

1502
00:58:20,296 --> 00:58:21,464
THE DANCE IS ON THE HOUSE

1503
00:58:21,531 --> 00:58:22,999
THE UNWANTED 2ND LIVE PERFORMANCE

1504
00:58:23,066 --> 00:58:25,702
Here to rip Poong's plastic pants in half

1505
00:58:25,768 --> 00:58:27,337
and own <i>Chef & My Fridge,</i>

1506
00:58:27,403 --> 00:58:29,706
Director Yoo Yong-wook.

1507
00:58:29,772 --> 00:58:31,274
Unorthodox versus unorthodox.

1508
00:58:31,341 --> 00:58:32,642
Street versus street.

1509
00:58:32,709 --> 00:58:34,477
Who's going to be the winner?

1510
00:58:34,544 --> 00:58:37,881
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1511
00:58:39,849 --> 00:58:40,917
Begin!

1512
00:58:42,185 --> 00:58:44,787
THE UNORTHODOX ROUND BEGINS

1513
00:58:45,555 --> 00:58:46,489
GREEN ONION

1514
00:58:46,556 --> 00:58:48,358
This is the fun kind of cook-off.

1515
00:58:48,424 --> 00:58:49,659
I knew it.

1516
00:58:49,893 --> 00:58:52,228
AS ALWAYS, POONG'S CHILI OIL

1517
00:58:52,295 --> 00:58:53,663
He doesn't like to eat green onion,

1518
00:58:53,730 --> 00:58:55,899
so I'll add a lot of aroma into the oil.

1519
00:58:57,500 --> 00:58:58,434
LET'S GO!

1520
00:58:58,501 --> 00:58:59,369
Let's go!

1521
00:59:00,036 --> 00:59:01,404
STRAIGHT TO THE GRILL!

1522
00:59:01,471 --> 00:59:02,305
PERILLA OIL

1523
00:59:02,372 --> 00:59:03,473
Right away, sesame oil.

1524
00:59:03,540 --> 00:59:05,408
-He's using oil?
-Yes.

1525
00:59:05,475 --> 00:59:07,243
Director Yong-wook

1526
00:59:07,310 --> 00:59:09,012
is pan-frying over the charcoal.

1527
00:59:09,078 --> 00:59:10,313
Is he?

1528
00:59:10,380 --> 00:59:12,916
-I see.
-There's a pan over the grill.

1529
00:59:13,216 --> 00:59:15,051
PEANUTS

1530
00:59:15,618 --> 00:59:16,786
Peanuts?

1531
00:59:16,853 --> 00:59:19,923
1. GRIND PEANUTS AND OLIVE OIL
TO MAKE PEANUT SPREAD

1532
00:59:19,989 --> 00:59:20,857
Are those peanuts?

1533
00:59:20,924 --> 00:59:22,959
Yes, he's grinding them with oil.

1534
00:59:23,860 --> 00:59:24,794
Okay. The spread.

1535
00:59:24,861 --> 00:59:26,930
-What's that?
-Peanut butter.

1536
00:59:26,996 --> 00:59:28,431
-Cashew nut spread.
-Cashew nut spread.

1537
00:59:28,498 --> 00:59:30,033
-He'll create smoke with that?
-In a blender?

1538
00:59:30,099 --> 00:59:31,434
No, I think that's the sauce.

1539
00:59:31,501 --> 00:59:33,069
How will you make smoke with sesame seeds?

1540
00:59:33,136 --> 00:59:35,305
Sesame seeds are rich in fat,

1541
00:59:35,371 --> 00:59:37,674
so I'm sure the smoke will be different.

1542
00:59:37,740 --> 00:59:39,609
It smells different
when there's fat in it.

1543
00:59:39,676 --> 00:59:41,578
And he's an expert in that area.

1544
00:59:41,644 --> 00:59:44,380
Next, kimchi juice.

1545
00:59:44,447 --> 00:59:46,349
-Kimchi?
-He's adding kimchi juice.

1546
00:59:46,583 --> 00:59:48,117
1. STRAIN KIMCHI JUICE THROUGH CHEESECLOTH

1547
00:59:48,184 --> 00:59:50,753
He's making a flour batter
with kimchi juice.

1548
00:59:50,820 --> 00:59:54,223
For the color? Are you making the pants?

1549
00:59:54,290 --> 00:59:55,892
-I am.
-The pink pants?

1550
00:59:55,959 --> 00:59:57,760
He's already making the pink pants.

1551
00:59:57,827 --> 00:59:58,861
-Yes.
-Pink.

1552
00:59:58,928 --> 01:00:00,597
Pink pants with kimchi juice?

1553
01:00:00,663 --> 01:00:02,932
-Where did he learn that?
-That must be starch.

1554
01:00:02,999 --> 01:00:04,901
That's the only way
the batter will turn clear.

1555
01:00:04,968 --> 01:00:06,102
You're right.

1556
01:00:06,169 --> 01:00:07,637
It's a mix of starch and flour.

1557
01:00:07,704 --> 01:00:10,340
There's a technique in Western cuisine
called crystal potato,

1558
01:00:10,406 --> 01:00:13,176
where you use starch
to make potato transparent.

1559
01:00:13,242 --> 01:00:14,477
TRANSPARENT GARNISH
MADE WITH POTATO STARCH

1560
01:00:14,544 --> 01:00:17,480
-He's using that technique?
-Starch turns clear when cooked.

1561
01:00:17,547 --> 01:00:19,082
He's rubbing something already.

1562
01:00:19,148 --> 01:00:21,684
-Butter.
-Butter?

1563
01:00:21,751 --> 01:00:23,586
-Is that for the pants?
-Yes.

1564
01:00:23,653 --> 01:00:25,521
Yes, I'll pour the starch into this.

1565
01:00:25,588 --> 01:00:29,459
-You steam starch water and do that.
-Like a sheet.

1566
01:00:29,525 --> 01:00:30,560
It's been three minutes.

1567
01:00:30,627 --> 01:00:31,861
You can probably see

1568
01:00:31,928 --> 01:00:33,162
hints of powder, right?

1569
01:00:33,229 --> 01:00:36,265
That's because he didn't strain it.
It shows how unorthodox he is.

1570
01:00:36,332 --> 01:00:38,668
FIERCE CRITICISM

1571
01:00:38,735 --> 01:00:40,870
Don't dismiss the cook
from the streets too much.

1572
01:00:40,937 --> 01:00:43,740
If he were a chef,
he would've strained it for a few seconds.

1573
01:00:43,806 --> 01:00:46,242
But he still has those lumps.

1574
01:00:46,309 --> 01:00:48,444
So Poong has a lot more to learn.

1575
01:00:48,511 --> 01:00:50,513
He's covering it with a wrap.

1576
01:00:52,582 --> 01:00:54,484
-It'll stick.
-It got stuck.

1577
01:00:54,550 --> 01:00:56,919
It got stuck! It's not supposed to stick!

1578
01:00:56,986 --> 01:00:59,322
I was so stressed out
whenever he did something like that!

1579
01:00:59,389 --> 01:01:01,924
-It's stuck!
-All right.

1580
01:01:02,358 --> 01:01:04,260
MIMICS THE POONGDOZER

1581
01:01:04,527 --> 01:01:06,663
GETTING A TASTE OF HIS OWN MEDICINE…

1582
01:01:06,729 --> 01:01:09,632
-Are you mimicking him?
-It's so annoying when he does this.

1583
01:01:09,866 --> 01:01:12,669
FIRST, INTO THE MICROWAVE!

1584
01:01:13,336 --> 01:01:15,271
-He grabs kimchi.
-Kimchi.

1585
01:01:15,338 --> 01:01:19,108
Yong-wook pulls out napa cabbage kimchi.

1586
01:01:19,175 --> 01:01:21,411
He's using the grill.

1587
01:01:21,477 --> 01:01:23,513
Is that pork neck?

1588
01:01:23,579 --> 01:01:24,947
Yes, I put pork neck on the grill.

1589
01:01:25,014 --> 01:01:26,149
And then…

1590
01:01:26,215 --> 01:01:29,252
-Sesame seeds are going in.
-He's raising the heat too.

1591
01:01:29,686 --> 01:01:30,620
POURING

1592
01:01:31,421 --> 01:01:32,822
SESAME SEEDS GO INTO THE FIRE!

1593
01:01:33,423 --> 01:01:35,324
I think he's creating smoke.

1594
01:01:35,391 --> 01:01:38,661
So smoke comes out
when sesame seeds go into charcoal.

1595
01:01:38,728 --> 01:01:40,563
It's rich in fat,
so it creates a lot of smoke.

1596
01:01:40,630 --> 01:01:42,598
-It does.
-It's making a lot.

1597
01:01:42,665 --> 01:01:45,101
You can create that much smoke
with sesame seeds.

1598
01:01:45,168 --> 01:01:46,669
-As they burn.
-Yes.

1599
01:01:48,438 --> 01:01:50,139
POTATO

1600
01:01:50,206 --> 01:01:51,407
MINCED GARLIC

1601
01:01:51,474 --> 01:01:52,542
Minced garlic.

1602
01:01:52,608 --> 01:01:53,443
-Pepper.
-One pepper.

1603
01:01:54,544 --> 01:01:55,978
And meat.

1604
01:01:56,045 --> 01:01:58,748
-And pork neck.
-It looks good.

1605
01:01:58,815 --> 01:02:02,218
-Cooking wine.
-Cooking wine. Soy sauce.

1606
01:02:02,285 --> 01:02:04,487
-Ten minutes.
-Kimchi!

1607
01:02:04,721 --> 01:02:07,090
ADDS KIMCHI TO THE BLENDER TOO!

1608
01:02:07,657 --> 01:02:10,493
He's grinding meat with kimchi.

1609
01:02:10,560 --> 01:02:14,397
You're grinding pork and kimchi together?

1610
01:02:14,464 --> 01:02:18,835
They don't look like pork and kimchi now.
He ground everything up.

1611
01:02:18,901 --> 01:02:19,936
Nine minutes.

1612
01:02:21,471 --> 01:02:23,573
Yong-wook looks surprised.

1613
01:02:23,639 --> 01:02:26,109
-Nine minutes!
-You might want to move a bit faster.

1614
01:02:26,175 --> 01:02:27,910
Wow.

1615
01:02:27,977 --> 01:02:30,480
You're losing time while chanting, "Wow."

1616
01:02:30,546 --> 01:02:32,515
3. MIX CONDIMENTS AND MAKE SEASONING

1617
01:02:32,582 --> 01:02:35,384
He's someone who can
cook barbecue even for 20 hours.

1618
01:02:35,451 --> 01:02:36,385
OPENING THE LID

1619
01:02:36,452 --> 01:02:38,054
He opens the grill lid.

1620
01:02:38,121 --> 01:02:40,490
The meat doesn't look too cooked.

1621
01:02:40,556 --> 01:02:43,292
-It's okay.
-Is he embracing it with smoke?

1622
01:02:43,359 --> 01:02:46,129
-Pork neck.
-He's going to marinade it.

1623
01:02:46,195 --> 01:02:49,298
-You're right.
-It looks nicely cooked at the bottom.

1624
01:02:49,365 --> 01:02:53,035
I hope the smoky flavor lasts
until he tries the dish,

1625
01:02:53,102 --> 01:02:57,240
but I'm not sure if it'll prevail
with the soy sauce marinade.

1626
01:02:57,306 --> 01:02:59,442
He's saying smoking
is unnecessary with soy sauce.

1627
01:02:59,509 --> 01:03:01,611
And next, some starch.

1628
01:03:01,677 --> 01:03:02,578
The mixture.

1629
01:03:02,645 --> 01:03:03,813
MIXING WITH THE GROUND FILLING

1630
01:03:03,880 --> 01:03:05,915
It looks like kimchi <i>mandu</i>,
dumplings with kimchi.

1631
01:03:06,315 --> 01:03:08,718
FIDDLING AND MAKING A SHAPE…

1632
01:03:08,785 --> 01:03:10,086
What are you…

1633
01:03:10,153 --> 01:03:11,420
What are you making?

1634
01:03:11,487 --> 01:03:12,989
-And now…
-Darn it.

1635
01:03:13,055 --> 01:03:15,792
-You don't have time.
-He just said, "Darn it."

1636
01:03:15,858 --> 01:03:18,060
Poong is going, "Darn it."
Something isn't going well.

1637
01:03:18,127 --> 01:03:19,195
What are you making?

1638
01:03:19,262 --> 01:03:21,597
They're like meatballs with kimchi.

1639
01:03:21,664 --> 01:03:24,567
I think those are the legs
that'll put on the pants.

1640
01:03:24,634 --> 01:03:27,003
-Those are the legs?
-They are.

1641
01:03:27,436 --> 01:03:29,071
LOOK AT THOSE DETAILS

1642
01:03:29,705 --> 01:03:30,973
BIPED

1643
01:03:31,040 --> 01:03:32,041
Aren't I right?

1644
01:03:32,108 --> 01:03:35,478
He's making plastic pants,
so the filling must be the legs.

1645
01:03:35,545 --> 01:03:37,246
So will it be three-dimensional?

1646
01:03:37,313 --> 01:03:39,782
-It's in 3D.
-It's in 3D.

1647
01:03:39,849 --> 01:03:42,451
J. Y. Park's legs are getting cooked.

1648
01:03:42,752 --> 01:03:44,387
IT SOUNDS RATHER GRUESOME

1649
01:03:44,453 --> 01:03:45,288
He's always improving.

1650
01:03:45,655 --> 01:03:47,523
HIS DEPTH IMPROVES FROM DAY TO DAY

1651
01:03:47,590 --> 01:03:49,926
Last time, he made a dinosaur.

1652
01:03:49,992 --> 01:03:51,727
He made a T-Rex.

1653
01:03:51,794 --> 01:03:52,995
And made it stand?

1654
01:03:53,062 --> 01:03:55,264
-No, like this. In 2D.
-It's a shock.

1655
01:03:56,866 --> 01:03:57,700
And then…

1656
01:03:58,501 --> 01:04:00,136
OPENING THE LID AGAIN

1657
01:04:00,203 --> 01:04:02,104
I don't think the meat will cook in time.

1658
01:04:02,171 --> 01:04:03,039
Mine?

1659
01:04:03,105 --> 01:04:04,640
-No, they will.
-Stay confident.

1660
01:04:04,707 --> 01:04:06,242
Will the temperature rise later?

1661
01:04:06,309 --> 01:04:08,978
It will get hotter like an oven.

1662
01:04:09,045 --> 01:04:11,080
But the grill isn't as hot as you think…

1663
01:04:11,647 --> 01:04:12,949
WHAT ABOUT PORK BELLY?

1664
01:04:13,015 --> 01:04:14,984
So he's not using the pork belly?

1665
01:04:15,051 --> 01:04:17,320
-He just used its fat.
-Just to cook the kimchi?

1666
01:04:17,386 --> 01:04:19,021
GRILLING JUST KIMCHI

1667
01:04:19,088 --> 01:04:22,491
It tastes delicious
when it's cooked in pork fat.

1668
01:04:22,558 --> 01:04:25,061
There's lots of grease in the kimchi now,

1669
01:04:25,127 --> 01:04:26,796
both perilla oil and lard.

1670
01:04:26,863 --> 01:04:29,465
He cooked kimchi
as he melted the pork belly fat.

1671
01:04:29,532 --> 01:04:31,701
I see. But he's not using
the pork belly itself.

1672
01:04:31,767 --> 01:04:33,669
Five minutes and forty seconds.

1673
01:04:33,736 --> 01:04:35,738
-Five minutes and forty seconds.
-Hurry.

1674
01:04:36,505 --> 01:04:38,174
REMOVING THE GRILL IN THE CENTER!

1675
01:04:38,241 --> 01:04:40,042
It's Yong-wook's first time here,

1676
01:04:40,109 --> 01:04:42,211
but he hears everything
and can even respond.

1677
01:04:42,278 --> 01:04:43,880
I guess he can hear us.

1678
01:04:43,946 --> 01:04:45,248
Gosh.

1679
01:04:45,314 --> 01:04:47,116
Sesame seeds.

1680
01:04:48,517 --> 01:04:49,485
WHAT'S THIS MACHINE?

1681
01:04:49,552 --> 01:04:51,053
That's a smoking gun!

1682
01:04:51,120 --> 01:04:53,389
-What's that?
-You're putting that in there?

1683
01:04:53,456 --> 01:04:55,524
It's a Western cuisine technique,
smoke injection.

1684
01:04:56,259 --> 01:04:59,195
You can add the smoky aroma
without having to put the food

1685
01:04:59,262 --> 01:05:01,731
directly over the charcoal.

1686
01:05:01,797 --> 01:05:03,299
Because that'll burn.

1687
01:05:04,767 --> 01:05:11,440
4. MIX TOFU NOODLES WITH ANCHOVY AEKJEOT,
VICTORY ONION SAUCE, PERILLA OIL, GARLIC

1688
01:05:11,807 --> 01:05:13,709
PREPARING SEASONED TOFU NOODLES TOO!

1689
01:05:13,976 --> 01:05:15,711
THE GROUND FILLING

1690
01:05:15,878 --> 01:05:17,280
THE REMAINDER GOES INTO ANOTHER PAN!

1691
01:05:18,047 --> 01:05:19,849
-Maple syrup.
-Maple syrup?

1692
01:05:19,916 --> 01:05:22,318
-What is that?
-Maple syrup goes in that?

1693
01:05:22,385 --> 01:05:24,387
-With kimchi?
-With kimchi?

1694
01:05:24,453 --> 01:05:27,690
-Even honey? That's too sweet.
-It's grain syrup.

1695
01:05:28,024 --> 01:05:29,725
SQUEEZE

1696
01:05:30,893 --> 01:05:32,461
That's too much.

1697
01:05:32,528 --> 01:05:34,964
That looks way too sweet.
What's he trying to do?

1698
01:05:35,031 --> 01:05:36,899
3. BOIL FILLING WITH MAPLE SYRUP,
GRAIN SYRUP, CHILI OIL, AND WATER

1699
01:05:36,966 --> 01:05:39,101
He's using <i>tteokbokki</i> rice cake.

1700
01:05:39,168 --> 01:05:42,271
Poong chops <i>tteok.</i>
J. Y. Park will love that flavor.

1701
01:05:42,338 --> 01:05:43,606
Now, turn it over.

1702
01:05:45,308 --> 01:05:47,443
-Are those burnt?
-They aren't!

1703
01:05:47,510 --> 01:05:50,112
-They weren't red, but black!
-Maillard reaction.

1704
01:05:50,179 --> 01:05:53,549
-They're burnt? He burnt those?
-No!

1705
01:05:53,616 --> 01:05:56,285
-It's the Maillard reaction. It's fine.
-He's making black pants.

1706
01:05:56,352 --> 01:05:58,054
I can't believe they're totally charred.

